Many objectives find a common formalization in the expected free energy (EFE) framework of active inference 1 . EFE can be decomposed into two terms: (i) risk –the KL divergence between predicted and preferred outcomes, capturing the expected cost of a policy relative to homeostatic, rewarding, and normative goals–and (ii) ambiguity –the expected entropy of observations given hidden states, capturing uncertainty in the mapping from (belief) states to outcomes 2 . By selecting policies that minimize EFE, agents automatically balance exploitation and exploration. Mathematically, for a policy π \pi , EFE is: G ( π ) = D K L [ Q ( o τ | π ) ∥ P ( o τ ) ] ⏟ risk (expected cost) + 𝔼 Q ( o τ , s τ | π ) [ H [ P ( o τ | s τ ) ] ] ⏟ ambiguity \begin{equation}
G(\pi) = \underbrace{D_{KL}[Q(o_\tau|\pi) \| P(o_\tau)]}_{\text{risk (expected cost)}} + \underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{Q(o_\tau, s_\tau|\pi)}[H[P(o_\tau|s_\tau)]]}_{\text{ambiguity}}
\end{equation} This equation makes the brain’s "objective" look like preference satisfaction + uncertainty reduction, expressed in a single control functional. The first term (risk) says: choose policies whose predicted future outcomes ( Q ( o τ | π ) Q(o_\tau|\pi) ) look like the outcomes the organism "wants" to encounter, where "want" is encoded as a prior preference distribution P ( o τ ) P(o_\tau) . In active inference, that preference distribution is where homeostatic setpoints, learned rewards, and social/normative goals all live–so value is not a separate add-on, but a prior over outcomes that action tries to realize. Concretely, minimizing D K L [ Q ( o τ | π ) | | P ( o τ ) ] D_{KL}[Q(o_\tau|\pi)||P(o_\tau)] is equivalent to minimizing expected "cost" (negative log preference). The second term (ambiguity) says: choose policies that visit states where the likelihood mapping P ( o τ | s τ ) P(o_\tau|s_\tau) is sharp (low-entropy)–i.e. where we know what observation(s) to expect when entering a given state. This term formalizes an epistemic drive: actions that reduce ambiguity are actions that (on average) improve state-estimation and world-model accuracy, yielding the explore/exploit tradeoff without bolting on a separate "curiosity bonus." Both terms emerge from a single variational principle–minimizing EFE is equivalent to maximizing model evidence (minimizing long-run surprise), so the framework inherits the mathematical rigor of variational Bayesian inference. The decomposition isn’t arbitrary: risk and ambiguity naturally separate when expanding the expected log probability of preferred outcomes, reflecting the fundamental distinction between getting what you want and knowing what’s going on . Importantly, EFE subsumes standard RL–under certain conditions (known dynamics, flat priors except for reward), EFE minimization reduces to expected reward maximization–making it a generalization that explains when reward-based models succeed and why they fail for information-seeking behaviors with no instrumental payoff. This formalization is important because it provides a single objective that (a) makes value precise as a prior over outcomes (so homeostasis, learned reward, and norms can live in one place), and (b) makes curiosity/epistemic action a first-class consequence of optimal control rather than a bolt-on bonus. That unification matters for brain theory: it explains why organisms routinely take actions that look "non-reward-maximizing" in the short term (checking, sampling, exploring, seeking clarity) while still serving long-run viability and goal attainment–because reducing ambiguity is instrumentally valuable for keeping the world-model calibrated and control stable. Evolution "bakes in" both EFE terms by shaping (a) what outcomes count as acceptable and (b) how much the organism should pay to be less wrong about hidden state before committing to action. The risk term is installed by hard-wiring a preference landscape over outcomes: there are steep fitness cliffs (hypoxia, hypoglycemia, dehydration, hyperthermia, injury, social expulsion) where small prediction errors can become irreversible. Brains that treat predicted proximity to those cliffs as intrinsically costly outcompete brains that don’t. Mechanistically, this shows up as deep "viability priors" implemented via interoceptive control loops (brainstem/hypothalamus/insula), threat and aversive-learning circuits (amygdala-PAG, extended amygdala, habenula), and basal ganglia gating that biases policy selection away from outcome distributions historically correlated with loss of viability. In EFE language, selection tunes P ( o τ ) P(o_\tau) (preferred outcomes) to concentrate probability mass on states compatible with survival/reproduction, so minimizing D K L [ Q ( o τ | π ) ∥ P ( o τ ) ] D_{KL}[Q(o_\tau|\pi) \| P(o_\tau)] becomes a generic way to avoid low-fitness regions of outcome space. The ambiguity term is installed because, for embodied agents, control is only as good as state estimation: actions chosen under a misidentified state can be catastrophically wrong (eat the wrong berry; approach the wrong silhouette; misread dominance cues). So evolution favors architectures that treat expected identifiability of the world as a first-class control variable. This yields brains built as hierarchical generative models whose default operation is to reduce prediction error via precision-weighted inference , plus a set of active-sensing "moves" (orienting reflexes, saccades, sniffing/whisking, head turns, information-seeking locomotion) that deliberately place the organism in states where the likelihood mapping is sharper. Neuromodulators serve the ambiguity term by (i) tuning precision weights (gain) on prediction-error pathways so the brain trusts the right evidence at the right level, (ii) gating plasticity to learn a sharper P ( o | s ) P(o|s) , and (iii) modulating policy precision and exploration regimes so the agent chooses actions that land it in states with more diagnostic observations. Evolution makes this possible with a very specific control architecture: a handful of small, phylogenetically old "hub" nuclei (brainstem/basal forebrain/midbrain) that (a) integrate high-level summary signals about surprise, arousal, interoceptive state, and action outcomes, then (b) broadcast slow control signals via massively branching, diffuse projections across cortex-thalamus-hippocampus-basal ganglia, using receptor families that are heterogeneously expressed by layer/cell-type/synapse so the same chemical broadcast can selectively change gain, learning rate, and gating in the right microcircuits.

In the absence of specific physiological deficits or external rewards, biological agents do not simply idle; they actively restructure their environment to increase the number of future states they can reliably access. This drive is formalized as empowerment 1 , 2 . Unlike the drive for compression (which seeks to understand the world), empowerment seeks to dominate the causal dynamics of the world. It operationalizes the intuition that a "good" state is not just a safe one, but one that offers the agent the maximum number of distinct, controllable futures–effectively, a drive to maximize the channel capacity of the actuator-to-sensor loop. Formally, empowerment is defined using information theory. The agent treats the environment as a communication channel: its actions are the "transmitted signal" and the resulting sensory changes are the "received signal." An agent is empowered when its actions have a high mutual information with future sensory states–meaning different actions lead to reliably distinguishable outcomes. For a current state s t s_t , a sequence of k k actions a k : t + k a_{k:t+k} , and the resulting future state s t + k s_{t+k} , empowerment is the maximum possible mutual information achievable by an optimal policy: ℰ ( s t ) = max p ( a ) I ( A t : t + k ; S t + k | s t ) = max p ( a ) ( H ( S t + k | s t ) − H ( S t + k | s t , A t : t + k ) ) \begin{equation}
\mathcal{E}(s_t) = \max_{p(a)} I(A_{t:t+k} ; S_{t+k} | s_t) = \max_{p(a)} \left( H(S_{t+k}|s_t) - H(S_{t+k}|s_t, A_{t:t+k}) \right)
\end{equation} This equation reveals two critical components of control. To maximize , the agent must maximize H ( S t + k | s t ) H(S_{t+k}|s_t) (the diversity of reachable futures) while minimizing H ( S t + k | s t , A t : t + k ) H(S_{t+k}|s_t,A_{t:t+k}) (the uncertainty of an outcome given a specific action). In plain English: "I want to be in a position where I can make many different things happen (high potential entropy), but where I know exactly which action causes which result (low conditional entropy/noise)." This makes empowerment a clean formalization of an intrinsic motivation for option value : an organism can pursue high-empowerment states without specifying any particular external goal, because high controllability is a prerequisite for all future goal satisfaction. In the empowerment literature, this is explicitly framed as a pseudo-utility that induces a preference for "states that allow the agent to change its future," and the limiting case of vanishing empowerment aligns with being effectively unable to act (the control channel degenerates) 3 . While the functional logic of empowerment is to maximize future optionality , its biological realization appears as a primitive drive for causal efficacy –an "effectance" motive that motivates exploration even in the absence of extrinsic payoff 4 , 5 ; "keeping options open" is the evolutionary why , but "dominating causal dynamics" is the behavioral how .

(3) Entropy estimation over continuous, high-dimensional state spaces. Both terms in the empowerment decomposition–the marginal entropy of reachable futures H ( S t + k ∣ s t ) H(S_{t+k} \mid s_t) and the conditional entropy H ( S t + k ∣ s t , A t : t + k ) H(S_{t+k} \mid s_t, A_{t:t+k}) –require evaluating entropies of distributions over the state space. For realistic sensorimotor systems, this state space is continuous and high-dimensional (joint angles, visual features, spatial coordinates, etc.), making exact entropy computation intractable. In practice, this demands two interacting capabilities: (a) a compressed, low-dimensional state representation that mitigates the curse of dimensionality (connecting empowerment computation directly to the compression/representation-learning objective discussed previously), and (b) an entropy estimation method that operates within that compressed space – e.g., analytic computation under parametric forward models, k-nearest-neighbor estimators, or learned variational bounds. (a) and (b) are not independent engineering choices but co-constrained : the geometry of the learned representation determines which estimation methods are tractable (a Gaussian latent space admits closed-form conditional entropies; a discrete codebook reduces the problem to combinatorial counting), and conversely, the desire for tractable entropy estimation can serve as an inductive bias on the compression scheme itself. For instance, if the forward model in latent space is trained to produce Gaussian predictive distributions, P ( z t + k ∣ z t , a t : t + k ) = 𝒩 ( μ θ ( z t , a t : t + k ) , Σ θ ( z t , a t : t + k ) ) , \begin{equation}
  P(z_{t+k} \mid z_t, a_{t:t+k}) = \mathcal{N}\bigl(\mu_\theta(z_t, a_{t:t+k}),\; \Sigma_\theta(z_t, a_{t:t+k})\bigr),
\end{equation} then the conditional entropy for any specific action sequence has a closed-form solution, H ( Z t + k ∣ z t , a t : t + k ) = 1 2 log ⁡ det ⁡ ( 2 π e Σ θ ( z t , a t : t + k ) ) , \begin{equation}
  H(Z_{t+k} \mid z_t, a_{t:t+k}) = \frac{1}{2} \log \det \bigl(2\pi e\, \Sigma_\theta(z_t, a_{t:t+k})\bigr),
\end{equation} and the full conditional entropy term can be obtained by averaging over the action distribution: H ( Z t + k ∣ z t , A t : t + k ) = 𝔼 p ( a t : t + k ) [ 1 2 log det ( 2 π e Σ θ ( z t , a t : t + k ) ) ] . \begin{equation}
  H(Z_{t+k} \mid z_t, A_{t:t+k}) = \mathbb{E}_{p(a_{t:t+k})} \left[ \frac{1}{2} \log \det \bigl(2\pi e\, \Sigma_\theta(z_t, a_{t:t+k})\bigr) \right].
\end{equation} The marginal entropy H ( Z t + k ∣ z t ) H(Z_{t+k} \mid z_t) is more demanding: it requires marginalizing over the (optimized) action distribution to obtain the mixture P ( z t + k ∣ z t ) = ∫ p * ( a t : t + k ) P ( z t + k ∣ z t , a t : t + k ) d a t : t + k , \begin{equation}
  P(z_{t+k} \mid z_t) = \int p^*(a_{t:t+k})\, P(z_{t+k} \mid z_t, a_{t:t+k})\, da_{t:t+k},
\end{equation} which is a mixture of Gaussians whose entropy has no general closed-form solution. However, tractable approximations exist: variational lower bounds, moment-matching to a single Gaussian ( H ≈ 1 2 log ⁡ det ⁡ ( 2 π e Σ mix ) H \approx \frac{1}{2}\log\det(2\pi e\, \Sigma_{\text{mix}}) where Σ mix \Sigma_{\text{mix}} is the covariance of the mixture), or Monte Carlo estimation of the log-density via the mixture’s explicit form. A discrete codebook representation (e.g., VQ-VAE) offers an alternative route entirely, reducing entropy estimation to combinatorial counting over code indices: H = − ∑ i p i log ⁡ p i H = -\sum_i p_i \log p_i . In either case, the compression architecture and the entropy estimator are co-designed : the representational format is chosen (or learned) precisely so that the empowerment-relevant quantities are cheap to compute. Neural architecture; how is it evolutionarily instantiated as an architectural prior? The brain’s practical route to entropies over S t + k S_{t+k} is not explicit − ! ∫ p log ⁡ p -!\int p\log p integration in pixel/joint space, but an architectural prior that (i) compresses sensorimotor state into task-relevant latents and (ii) represents (and can rapidly manipulate) uncertainty/precision within those latents. Sensory cortical hierarchies perform massive, progressive dimensionality reduction: 10 6 10^6 retinal ganglion axons project through LGN to V1, which then feeds a cascade of extrastriate areas extracting increasingly abstract, lower-dimensional features (edges -> textures -> object parts -> object identity). Analogous hierarchies exist in somatosensory, auditory, and proprioceptive pathways. By the time sensory information reaches associative and prefrontal regions–where action-relevant state evaluation plausibly occurs–the representational space has been compressed by orders of magnitude relative to the raw receptor sheet. This compression is not generic: it is shaped by evolutionary priors (genetically specified cytoarchitectonic boundaries, laminar connectivity motifs, topographic gradients established by molecular cues like ephrins) that constrain the cortical hierarchy to extract the sensorimotor-relevant degrees of freedom –object positions, body-configuration variables, agent-relevant affordances–while discarding task-irrelevant variation. This is precisely the kind of compression that renders downstream entropy estimation tractable. Within these compressed representations, neural populations appear to encode not point estimates of state but distributional information–probability distributions over latent state variables–through what have been termed Probabilistic Population Codes (PPCs) (see "Representing Uncertainty"). In such codes, the pattern of firing rates across a population specifies the parameters of an approximate (often near-Gaussian) posterior distribution, while trial-to-trial variability and noise correlations carry information about uncertainty. This format is significant because it makes entropy-related quantities directly "readable" by downstream circuits: the width or covariance of the encoded distribution–which is monotonically related to its entropy–is available in the population activity pattern itself, without requiring an explicit logarithmic computation. For approximately Gaussian population codes, the conditional entropy H ( Z t + k | z t , a t : t + k ) H(Z_{t+k}|z_t,a_{t:t+k}) is determined (up to a constant) by the log-determinant of the predictive covariance, which can in principle be read out from the gain or dispersion of the population response encoding predicted future states. Forward models in motor-related circuits (across the hierarchy) produce predictions about future states with associated uncertainty, providing exactly the predictive distributions P ( z t + k | z t , a t : t + k ) P(z_{t+k}|z_t,a_{t:t+k}) whose spread determines the conditional entropy term. For the marginal entropy H ( Z t + k | z t ) H(Z_{t+k}|z_t) –requiring marginalization over actions–the brain need not compute the entropy of the full mixture analytically. Instead, a population that aggregates predictions across multiple candidate action sequences (e.g., via parallel cortico-basal ganglia loops evaluating different action candidates) would exhibit compound variability reflecting both the per-action predictive uncertainty and the across-action spread of predicted outcomes. The total variance (or second-moment statistics) of such a population implicitly encodes a moment-matched approximation to the mixture entropy. This is the neural analogue of the ∑ m i x \sum_{mix} approximation described above: downstream circuits can read the marginal entropy as the overall dispersion of predicted-future-state representations, without ever explicitly representing or integrating over the mixture density. The key architectural point is that evolution did not separately optimize a compression scheme and an entropy estimator; it shaped cortical representations so that the format of the compressed code is simultaneously the substrate for entropy readout. The approximately Gaussian, smoothly topographic character of many cortical population codes is not merely a consequence of efficient coding for perception–it is also an evolved architectural prior that makes predictive-uncertainty quantities (and thereby empowerment) cheap to extract. The convergence of anatomical projections from sensory hierarchies, cerebellar forward-model outputs, and action-candidate representations onto common integrative regions (posterior parietal cortex, dorsolateral prefrontal cortex) instantiates the co-design principle at the level of neural circuitry: the compression, prediction, and entropy-estimation functions are physically wired to interact.

Before examining how the cortex computes and propagates error signals, we must specify what it is optimizing–the objective functions or "loss functions" whose minimization defines what "good" networks accomplish. Talking about a loss function for cortex is necessarily a normative move: cortical tissue does not (and need not) compute a single scalar objective and backpropagate its gradient. Instead, the cortex implements a family of locally computable discrepancy measures whose combined effect can be interpreted as optimizing an implicit objective. Several overlapping normative proposals have accumulated over the past half-century–redundancy reduction, efficient coding, sparse coding, slow feature analysis, infomax, prediction error minimization–that might at first appear to be competing theories. A central theoretical achievement of the last two decades has been to show that most of these can be understood as components of, or special cases of, a single unifying objective: the minimization of variational free energy in a hierarchical generative model 1 , 2 , 3 . A complementary unifying move is that cortical prediction is inherently sensorimotor : perception is an action-based hypothesis-testing process, so predictions (and hence local losses) are conditioned not only on latent causes but also on impending and executed actions (via efference copy and feedback). This motivates active predictive coding (APC) as a cortical loss framing in which both sensory and action prediction errors can drive inference and learning 4 . The remainder of this section unpacks models of core cortical loss functions, identifies the additional implicit costs (metabolic, homeostatic) that shape the biological loss landscape, and examines how the effective loss varies across cortical areas, timescales, and neuromodulatory states. The most comprehensive normative account of cortical learning casts it as approximate Bayesian inference in a hierarchical generative model of the sensorium 1 , 2 , 5 , 6 . The cortex is hypothesized to maintain an internal generative model p θ ( x , z 1 , . . . , z L ) p_\theta(x,z_1,...,z_L) that specifies how latent causes z l z_l at multiple hierarchical levels conspire to generate sensory observations x x . Because exact Bayesian posterior inference–computing p ( z ∣ x ) p(z∣x) –is intractable for any model of realistic complexity, the cortex instead maintains an approximate posterior q ϕ ( z | x ) q_\phi(z|x) , parameterized by neural activity patterns (for fast online inference) and synaptic weights (for slow model learning), and minimizes the variational free energy: F = D K L [ q ϕ ( z ∣ x ) ∥ p θ ( z ) ] ⏟ complexity − 𝔼 q ϕ [ log ⁡ p θ ( x ∣ z ) ] ⏟ accuracy \begin{equation}
  F \;=\; \underbrace{D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\bigl[q_\phi(z \mid x)\;\|\;p_\theta(z)\bigr]}_{\text{complexity}} \;-\; \underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{q_\phi}\!\bigl[\log p_\theta(x \mid z)\bigr]}_{\text{accuracy}}
\end{equation} The first term ( complexity ) is the information/description-length cost of encoding x x into latents z z –how far the posterior q ( z ∣ x ) q(z∣x) must move away from the prior p ( z ) p(z) (i.e., how many extra nats/bits the representation uses beyond what the model expects a priori). The second term ( accuracy ) is the model’s expected fit to the data under the inferred latents–how well samples z ∼ q ( z | x ) z\sim q(z|x) can predict/reconstruct x x (equivalently, its negative is the expected reconstruction error / negative log-likelihood). Since F ≥ − log ⁡ p θ ( x ) F\ge-\log{p_\theta(x)} (variational free energy upper-bounds surprisal, equivalently negative log-model-evidence) minimizing F F simultaneously tightens the variational bound (making q ϕ q_\phi a better approximation to the true posterior) and increases model evidence (making p θ p_\theta a better model of the data). In this framework, cortical inference and cortical learning are two faces of the same optimization–free energy minimization–operating on different timescales: fast neural dynamics update activity patterns to minimize F F for fixed weights (inference/perception, on the order of hundreds of milliseconds), while slower synaptic plasticity updates weights to minimize F F averaged across data (learning, on the order of minutes to years) 1 , 2 , 6 . Decomposition into precision-weighted prediction errors Under the Laplace approximation (Gaussian posteriors parameterized by their means, with estimated covariance structure) and a hierarchical generative model in which each level predicts the level below via x l ̂ = f θ ( z t + 1 ) \hat{x_l}=f_\theta(z_{t+1}) , the variational free energy decomposes into a sum of local, precision-weighted prediction-error terms 7 , 1 , 8 : F ≈ ∑ l = 0 L [ 1 2 𝜺 l ⊤ 𝚷 l 𝜺 l + 1 2 ln | 𝚷 l − 1 | ] \begin{equation}
  F \;\approx\; \sum_{l=0}^{L} \left[\frac{1}{2}\,\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_l^\top\,\boldsymbol{\Pi}_l\,\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_l \;+\; \frac{1}{2}\ln\!\bigl|\boldsymbol{\Pi}_l^{-1}\bigr|\right]
\end{equation} where 𝜺 l = 𝒙 𝒍 − 𝒙 𝒍 ̂ \boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_l=\boldsymbol{x_l}-\boldsymbol{\hat{x_l}} is the prediction error at level l l (the mismatch between the representation at that level and the top-down prediction generated by level l + 1 l+1 ), and 𝚷 l \boldsymbol{\Pi}_l is the precision matrix (inverse covariance) encoding the expected reliability of predictions at that level. The first term within each summand is the precision-weighted squared prediction error: large, high-precision mismatches incur a heavy cost and drive strong updates, while equally large but low-precision mismatches (attributed to noise) incur little cost. The second term–the log-determinant of the covariance–penalizes overconfident precision estimates, preventing the degenerate solution of simply setting all precisions to zero (which would trivially zero-out the first term by declaring all predictions unreliable, but would incur unbounded cost under the second term). Though this log-determinant term is frequently omitted in simplified treatments, it is normatively essential for a self-consistent system that must learn both the content of its predictions and their reliability 1 , 9 (precision is covered in depth in the next section ("Cortical Error Signals")). The critical computational insight is that the global free energy decomposes into a sum of local losses , each defined at a single hierarchical level using only locally available quantities: the top-down prediction from the level above and the bottom-up representation at the current level. This level-wise decomposition is the feature that makes hierarchical predictive coding biologically plausible as a cortical learning principle. No single neuron or cortical area needs to compute, store, or have access to the global loss; each area simply needs to compare its current representation with predictions arriving via feedback connections from hierarchically adjacent areas, compute a local mismatch, and adjust synaptic weights to reduce that mismatch. The aggregate effect of these local updates, carried out across all levels simultaneously, is to reduce the global free energy. The precision matrices 𝚷 l \boldsymbol{\Pi}_l appearing in Eq. 2 are not fixed hyperparameters but estimated quantities encoding the expected reliability (inverse variance) of predictions at each hierarchical level; functionally, they act as a gain field over error units, amplifying "trustworthy" mismatches and down-weighting those attributable to noise or irrelevant degrees of freedom 10 , 1 , 8 . The cortex therefore does not simply "reduce prediction error" but "reduce precision-weighted prediction error," so the effective loss landscape is continually reshaped by inferred uncertainty. Several partially redundant biological mechanisms–neuromodulatory gain control, apical dendritic gating, oscillatory synchrony, population-level variability suppression, and thalamocortical precision gating via the pulvinar–implement this precision weighting on overlapping timescales ranging from tens of milliseconds to minutes (details deferred to the "Cortical Error Signals" section). Their joint action ensures that the cortical loss function is inherently adaptive and context-dependent : the same magnitude of prediction error 𝜺 l \boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_l can drive vigorous synaptic updating in one behavioral state and be effectively ignored in another, depending on the precision assigned by these converging mechanisms. This precision-weighting framework has also been proposed as the computational basis of attention : attending to a stimulus or modality corresponds to increasing 𝚷 l \boldsymbol{\Pi}_l for the corresponding prediction-error units, thereby amplifying their influence on both fast inference and slow synaptic learning–recasting spatial, feature-based, and expectation-driven attentional modulation as different patterns of precision deployment across the cortical hierarchy 10 , 11 , 12 . Temporal framing A static, instantaneous prediction-error loss is insufficient for cortical learning. The world is not a succession of independent snapshots; environmental causes evolve continuously according to dynamical laws that span a vast range of timescales–from the millisecond acoustics of a phoneme to the minutes-long arc of a social narrative to the seasonal rhythms that shape foraging and migration. Kiebel, Daunizeau, and Friston proposed that cortical anatomy recapitulates the temporal hierarchy inherent in the dynamics of environmental states: the lowest levels correspond to fast fluctuations associated with sensory processing, whereas the highest levels encode slow contextual changes under which faster representations unfold 13 . A cortex that merely minimized the mismatch between its current prediction and the current sensory state–with no representation of how those states change –would be perpetually surprised by any structured temporal sequence, unable to distinguish an expected continuation from a genuine novelty. The loss function must therefore be extended along the temporal axis in at least three complementary ways: (i) the generative model must encode dynamics –not only "what" but "what next" and "how fast"; (ii) the hierarchical decomposition of the loss must assign different levels to different temporal scales; and (iii) the system must, in some regimes, look forward in time, evaluating anticipated future prediction errors to guide action selection and policy optimization. The first extension amounts to replacing a static generative model with a hierarchical dynamical model –one whose hidden states have equations of motion and whose predictions therefore concern not just the current value of sensory input but its trajectory . Formally, this replaces a static likelihood p θ ( x ∣ z ) p_\theta(x\mid z) with a state-space (or controlled state-space) model: z t + 1 = f θ ( z t , a t ) + ω t , x t = g θ ( z t ) + ν t , \begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
z_{t+1} &= f_\theta(z_t, a_t) + \omega_t, \\
x_t &= g_\theta(z_t) + \nu_t,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation} where a t a_t denotes action, and ω t , ν t \omega_t,\nu_t are process and observation noise 14 . Importantly, the inclusion of a t a_t in the state-transition function is not merely a convenience for modeling overtly motor tasks; recent evidence suggests it is a canonical feature of cortical computation. Rao proposed active predictive coding (APC) , which posits that every cortical area maintains both a state-transition network f ̂ s \hat{f}_s (predicting the next latent state given the current state and action) and an action-prediction (policy) network f ̂ a \hat{f}_a (mapping state estimates to actions or abstract goals) 4 . This is motivated by converging empirical findings showing that action signals are ubiquitous across cortical areas: widefield calcium imaging reveals bilateral activation across cortical areas—including primary visual cortex—preceding movement on choice trials 15 ; spiking activity in nearly all recorded cortical regions contains neurons whose firing is predictive of upcoming movements 16 ; and approximately one-third of population activity variance in mouse visual cortex can be predicted from ongoing motor behavior 17 . Even at the laminar level, both L2/3 and L5/6 neurons in mouse V1 depolarize before locomotion onset, with layer-specific differences consistent with visuomotor comparison (L2/3) versus integration (L5/6) 18 . These observations support the APC claim that the action term a t a_t in the cortical generative model is not restricted to traditional motor areas but is present at every level of the cortical hierarchy, making the state-transition function f s ( s t − 1 , a t − 1 ) f_s(s_{t-1}, a_{t-1}) a canonical cortical computation rather than an optional extension for motor control. Friston and Kiebel generalized hierarchical predictive coding to cover dynamics in the world by formulating generative models in generalized coordinates of motion , which lends them a hierarchical form in both structure and dynamics 11 . In generalized coordinates, the brain represents not just the immediate observation state but all time derivatives: position, velocity, acceleration, jerk, and so on 19 . Equivalently, observations and hidden states are represented as generalized vectors: x ̃ t := ( x t , x ̇ t , x ̈ t , … ) , z ̃ t := ( z t , z ̇ t , z ̈ t , … ) . \begin{equation}
\tilde{x}_t := (x_t,\dot{x}_t,\ddot{x}_t,\ldots), \qquad \tilde{z}_t := (z_t,\dot{z}_t,\ddot{z}_t,\ldots).
\end{equation} Under a Laplace (Gaussian) approximation to the posterior, the resulting free energy decomposes into prediction errors over these generalized orders of motion, each weighted by a corresponding precision that encodes the expected smoothness of the dynamics at that order 11 , 10 , 9 . Under this approximation, free energy can be expressed as a sum over hierarchical levels l l and derivative orders k k : F ≈ ∑ l ∑ k ≥ 0 [ 1 2 ε l ( k ) ⊤ Π l ( k ) ε l ( k ) + 1 2 ln | Π l ( k ) − 1 | ] , \begin{equation}
F \;\approx\; \sum_{l}\sum_{k\ge 0}\left[\frac{1}{2}\,{\varepsilon}_{l}^{(k)\top}\,\Pi_{l}^{(k)}\,{\varepsilon}_{l}^{(k)} \;+\; \frac{1}{2}\ln\!\bigl|\Pi_{l}^{(k)-1}\bigr|\right],
\end{equation} where ε l ( k ) {\varepsilon}_{l}^{(k)} denotes the prediction error for the k k -th temporal derivative at level l l . The update equations become a form of generalized Bayesian filtering : neural dynamics track a moving target through generalized coordinates, and under linear-Gaussian assumptions predictive-coding updates recover Kalman-Bucy-style filtering dynamics (i.e precision-weighted prediction-error correction as a filter) 11 . The practical consequence is that temporal prediction errors–mismatches between expected and observed rates of change –become additional terms in the cortical loss, and they recruit the same hierarchical error-correction machinery (superficial pyramidal error units, deep-layer prediction units) that was described for instantaneous prediction. Simulations of this scheme show, for instance, a vigorous expression of prediction error after an expected sensory event is omitted, reflecting the dynamical nature of the recognition process: the model continues to predict what should have come next, even in the absence of input 11 . This omission-response phenomenology has been supported empirically: human auditory cortex and frontal regions exhibit a hierarchical organization of prediction and novelty-detection systems, with early mismatch negativity (MMN) reflecting local auditory predictions and later P3b responses reflecting more global, temporally integrative violations of expectation 20 . Intracranial recordings have directly demonstrated this hierarchy: temporal cortex responds to any change in immediate stimulus history, whereas frontal cortex responds exclusively to globally unpredictable changes spanning longer timescales 21 . The second extension–the assignment of different hierarchical levels to different temporal scales–connects the formal structure of the loss to the large-scale anatomy of the cortex. The environment is composed of coupled dynamical processes whose characteristic rates span orders of magnitude. In the framework of Kiebel et al., the generative model is itself hierarchical in time: fast sensory fluctuations are generated by low-level attractors whose dynamics are modulated by the states of slower, higher-level attractors, which in turn are controlled by still slower contextual variables 13 . Empirical evidence for temporal receptive windows (TRWs). Hasson and colleagues introduced the concept of the temporal receptive window –the duration over which accumulated sensory information influences a cortical area’s response–and showed that it increases systematically from sensory to association cortex 22 . Early visual cortex exhibited high response reliability regardless of disruptions in temporal structure, whereas higher areas were sensitive to temporal scrambling 22 . Lerner et al. extended this to auditory-language cortex using temporally scrambled narratives: early auditory cortex (A1) was driven by momentary input regardless of scrambling condition, intermediate areas required sentence-level coherence, and apex areas (parietal and frontal cortex) responded reliably only when intact paragraphs were heard in meaningful sequence–suggesting that the timescale of processing is a general organizing principle of the human cerebral cortex 23 . Importantly, superposition of visual and auditory TRW maps revealed that this orderly hierarchy is a general topographic organization principle of the human cortex, spanning modalities 23 . During spoken narrative comprehension, a fixed temporal sequence of response lags–on the scale of several seconds–propagates from early auditory cortex to language areas, the attention network, and finally the default mode network, directly demonstrating hierarchical information flow along the cortical timescale gradient 24 . Intrinsic neural timescales (INTs). A crucial advance beyond stimulus-driven TRWs was the discovery by Murray et al. that the timescale hierarchy is not merely imposed by task structure but is an intrinsic property of cortical dynamics. They measured timescales of intrinsic fluctuations in spiking activity across macaque cortical areas and found a hierarchical ordering, with sensory and prefrontal areas exhibiting shorter and longer timescales, respectively–suggesting that intrinsic timescales reflect areal specialization for computations over multiple temporal ranges 25 . These intrinsic timescales set the duration over which a neural circuit integrates its inputs: shorter timescales in sensory areas enable rapid tracking of dynamic stimuli, whereas prefrontal areas can integrate information over longer timescales to improve signal-to-noise in working memory and decision-making 25 . Temporal specialization arises from a region’s intrinsic dynamics–dynamics present even in the absence of direct sensory stimulation –revealing a hierarchical progression from fast timescales in sensory areas to slower timescales in association areas 26 . Moreover, neuronal timescales are not static: transmembrane current timescales across association regions increase during working memory delay periods, and working memory performance is predicted by the extent of timescale increase in prefrontal cortex 27 . Multiple timescales may arise from recurrent spatial connectivity within a cortical area and flexibly change with cognitive state: attention can lengthen the slow intrinsic timescale in V4, and these changes correlate with behavioral performance 28 . But what determines the intrinsic timescale of a cortical area? A convergent picture has emerged from anatomy, transcriptomics, and computational modeling, pointing to at least four interacting mechanisms: Gradient of recurrent excitatory synaptic strength. Chaudhuri et al. developed a large-scale dynamical model of macaque neocortex in which both local and inter-regional circuit mechanisms contributed to a hierarchy of timescales; a particularly important feature was that regions higher in the cortical hierarchy were endowed with stronger local excitatory connection strength, motivated by the empirical observation that pyramidal neurons in these regions possess a greater number of dendritic spines 29 , 30 , 31 . A hierarchy of timescales naturally emerged from this system: sensory areas showed brief, transient responses (appropriate for sensory processing), whereas association areas integrated inputs over time and exhibited persistent activity (suitable for decision-making and working memory) 30 . Mechanistically, stronger recurrent connections slow intrinsic dynamics by partially canceling leak currents, and there are confirmed increases across the cortical hierarchy in the number and density of excitatory synapses onto pyramidal cells, likely reflecting increased recurrent strength across areas 25 , 31 . NMDA receptor composition and synaptic kinetics. NMDA receptors in the recurrent circuits of prefrontal cortex exhibit roughly twofold longer decay time-constants than those in primary visual cortex and temporally summate trains of stimuli more effectively 32 . More generally, intrinsic timescale appears to depend not just on the amount of NMDA-mediated recurrence, but on its subunit composition : higher-order cortex shows hierarchy-linked enrichment of GRIN2B /GluN2B-related excitation 33 , 34 , and GluN2B-containing NMDARs deactivate much more slowly than GluN2A-containing receptors while coupling especially strongly to CaMKII-dependent plasticity signaling 35 , 36 . This helps explain why persistent activity in association cortex is thought to arise from attractor states sustained by NMDA-mediated recurrent excitation coupled with fast feedback inhibition 27 , 37 . The implication for learning is direct: areas with slower, more GluN2B-weighted NMDA kinetics implement a broader temporal integration kernel, so their prediction-error signals and eligibility traces can accumulate discrepancies over longer windows before being converted into synaptic change. Inter-areal connectivity and network architecture. The Chaudhuri et al. model was built on quantitative directed and weighted connectivity data, and the hierarchy of timescales emerged from the combination of local heterogeneity and specific inter-areal projection profiles; moreover, slower prefrontal and temporal areas had a disproportionate impact on global brain dynamics 30 . Importantly, the synaptic dynamics of backward connections have slower time constants; forward connections are driving and elicit obligatory responses, whereas backward connections have both driving and modulatory effects and operate over larger spatial and temporal scales 11 . This asymmetry is exactly what the temporal free-energy framework requires: feedback predictions encode slowly changing contextual priors that constrain faster inference at the levels below. Cell-intrinsic and neuromodulatory properties. A longer intrinsic timescale can also reflect timescales of cellular or synaptic dynamics; consistent with this, there are interareal differences in the dynamical properties of recurrent excitatory synapses, including differential composition of glutamate receptors, expression of short-term synaptic plasticity, and level of neuromodulation, as well as interareal differences in cellular physiology driven by factors such as neuronal morphology 25 . Areal specialization for timescales of learning is potentially shaped by gradients in the densities of receptors for neuromodulators including dopamine and serotonin 38 , 39 . Spike-frequency adaptation, voltage-gated ion channels with diverse time constants, and the balance of tonic versus phasic inhibition all contribute additional degrees of freedom. In sum, the cortical hierarchy of intrinsic timescales is not a single-mechanism phenomenon but an emergent property of graded anatomy (spine density, NMDA composition, myelination), large-scale directed connectivity, and cell-intrinsic biophysics—all of which are themselves organized along the macroscopic principal gradient from sensory to transmodal cortex. The third temporal extension of the loss function concerns the future . Standard variational free energy scores the fit between the generative model and past and present observations (i.e between priors and present beliefs); it is backward-looking by construction. But organisms must also evaluate policies –sequences of prospective actions–in terms of their anticipated consequences. Active inference addresses this by positing that the brain uses its generative model to predict incoming sensory data, and the fit between model and data may be improved either by optimizing beliefs (perceptual inference) or by acting on the world to change the data; this implies a common objective (minimizing variational free energy) for both action and perception 40 . Policy selection becomes a form of Bayesian model selection, where the evidence for a particular policy is the free energy expected in the future–an unusual objective because it scores policies based on outcomes that have yet to be observed 40 . This is typically expressed by defining the expected free energy (EFE) of a policy π \pi at future time τ \tau : G τ ( π ) := 𝔼 q ( o τ , s τ ∣ π ) [ ln q ( s τ ∣ π ) − ln p ( o τ , s τ ) ] , \begin{equation}
G_\tau(\pi) \;:=\; \mathbb{E}_{q(o_\tau,s_\tau\mid \pi)}\!\left[\ln q(s_\tau\mid \pi)\;-\;\ln p(o_\tau,s_\tau)\right],
\end{equation} with policy selection often written as q ( π ) ∝ exp ⁡ ( − γ ∑ τ G τ ( π ) ) q(\pi)\propto \exp(-\gamma\,\sum_\tau G_\tau(\pi)) for an inverse-temperature γ \gamma . The expected free energy is a central quantity in active inference: agents select policies that minimize G ( π ) G(\pi) , and its decomposition captures the exploration-exploitation tradeoff 41 . A common decomposition separates an epistemic value term (expected information gain about hidden states) from an extrinsic/pragmatic value term induced by prior preferences over outcomes p ( o τ ) p(o_\tau) ; outcomes are "valuable" insofar as they are assigned high prior probability by these preferences, and "costly" insofar as they are surprising under them 42 . This forward-looking extension of the loss has direct implications for the cortical timescale hierarchy: agents decide between temporally deep policies, where temporal depth refers to the depth of the planning horizon 40 . Prefrontal and default-mode regions–those at the apex of the temporal receptive window hierarchy–are the natural candidates for representing expected free energy over extended planning horizons, consistent with their role in prospective thinking, counterfactual reasoning, and goal-directed behavior (EFE discussed more in the "Superordinate Objectives" section). It is worth emphasizing that the expected free energy is not a second, independent objective: it is the natural forward-looking extension of the same variational quantity defined in Eq. 1. Structurally, both VFE and EFE share the generic form 𝔼 q [ ln ⁡ q − ln ⁡ p ] \mathbb{E}_q[\ln q-\ln p] ; the crucial difference lies in what grounds the expectation. VFE is conditioned on actual observations x x already received: it scores how well current beliefs q ϕ ( z | x ) q_\phi(z|x) explain real data, and is minimized by perceptual inference and synaptic learning. EFE, by contrast, is conditioned on a policy π \pi and taken over predicted future observations o τ o_\tau and states s τ s_\tau that the generative model expects to encounter if that policy is executed: it scores the anticipated quality of those encounters, and is minimized by policy selection. (Here, o τ o_\tau and s τ s_\tau correspond to the observations x and latent states z of the VFE formulation, now indexed by a future time τ and conditioned on an action sequence.) A second, substantive difference is that the joint model p ( o τ , s τ ) p(o_\tau,s_\tau) entering EFE incorporates prior preferences p ( o τ ) p(o_\tau) –an explicit specification of which sensory outcomes the agent treats as desirable–that have no direct analogue in the backward-looking VFE, which merely fits a generative model to whatever data arrive. It is these prior preferences that inject goal-directedness into policy selection: without them, EFE would reduce to pure epistemic foraging (maximizing information gain), with no drive toward biologically or task-relevant outcomes. Parr and Friston formalized the connection between VFE and EFE through the generalised free energy , which unifies the two within a single objective that transitions smoothly from assimilating present evidence (VFE-dominant) to evaluating potential futures (EFE-dominant) 40 . Operationally, the two quantities interlock in a perception-action cycle: EFE-based policy selection determines the agent’s next action; that action generates new sensory data; VFE minimization then assimilates those data into updated beliefs; and the cycle repeats–making VFE and EFE two temporally complementary faces of a single imperative to minimize surprise across time. A further temporal-framing move is chunking : rather than treating experience as a purely continuous stream, cortex appears to infer discrete latent "events" with relatively stable internal statistics, separated by boundaries where latent causes change. Data-driven neural-state segmentation during narrative perception reveals a nested event hierarchy : short events in sensory regions and longer events in high-order regions that encode abstract situation models 43 . This suggests a complementary picture to generalized-coordinate tracking: cortical hierarchies may combine (i) continuous-time trajectory tracking within an event with (ii) discrete latent-state transitions at event boundaries, where large prediction errors can trigger model reconfiguration and accelerated updating. In synthesis, the cortical loss function is not a single, static, instantaneous error signal. It is a temporally deep, hierarchically factorized objective that encompasses: (i) instantaneous prediction errors at each hierarchical level, weighted by level-specific precisions; (ii) dynamical prediction errors over generalized coordinates of motion, penalizing mismatches in the predicted trajectory, not just the current state; (iii) a hierarchy of temporal scales, anchored by the intrinsic timescale gradient of cortical areas, such that each level of the hierarchy is "assigned" to minimize prediction error over a characteristic temporal window; (iv) an anticipated or expected free energy that extends the loss into the future, guiding action and policy selection toward states that are both information-rich (epistemically valuable) and consistent with prior preferences (pragmatically valuable); and (v) event-level discretization that compresses long streams into latent-state segments. This is how a predictive cortex can remain locally error-driven while still being genuinely temporally deep . From Hierarchical Levels to Heterarchical Graphs The preceding paragraphs have formalized the cortical loss as a sum over hierarchical levels: each level sends predictions downward and receives prediction errors upward. This is a powerful abstraction, but it is not the only (or even the most faithful) way to formalize what cortex is doing. The anatomical reality is a dense, recurrent, skip-connected, multi-area graph with parallel cortico-cortical and cortico-thalamo-cortical pathways, abundant lateral connections within areas, and skip connections that routinely bypass intermediate levels (e.g via transthalamic cortico-pulvino-cortical routing 44 ). A useful generalization is therefore: cortex is a distributed inference-and-learning engine that performs variational message passing on a structured graphical model , where "hierarchies" are a special case –a convenient coordinate system imposed on a richer heterarchical substrate 45 , 46 . Nothing in the core computational idea of predictive coding– compare locally predicted activity against locally observed activity; pass mismatches; iterate until consistency –requires a chain-like hierarchy. One can write a generative model over any directed graph (including loopy graphs with lateral and skip connections), perform inference by iterative "relaxation" (coordinate descent on a variational objective), and update synapses with local Hebbian-like rules driven by pre/post activity and local errors. Recent work makes this explicit: predictive-coding-style inference and learning can be defined on arbitrary graph topologies , i.e., on the kind of heterarchical wiring patterns that real neocortex exhibits 47 , and can approximate (and, in certain limits, converge to) backpropagation-style gradients using only local information 48 (framed elsewhere as "reverse-mode differentiation" on arbitrary computation graphs 49 ). Concretely, the global free energy generalizes from a sum over hierarchical levels to a sum over nodes in a directed graph: F = ∑ i ∈ 𝒱 F i , F i = 1 2 𝜺 i ⊤ 𝚷 i 𝜺 i + 1 2 ln ⁡ | 𝚷 i − 1 | , \begin{equation}
  F \;=\; \sum_{i \in \mathcal{V}} F_i, \qquad
  F_i \;=\; \tfrac{1}{2}\,\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_i^\top\,
  \boldsymbol{\Pi}_i\,\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_i
  \;+\; \tfrac{1}{2}\ln\!\bigl|\boldsymbol{\Pi}_i^{-1}\bigr|,
\end{equation} where each node i i ’s local loss F i F_i is determined entirely by signals available at its afferents, and the graph ( 𝒱 , ℰ ) (\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E}) can include lateral, skip, and recurrent edges without altering the form of the local update. This local computability is precisely what distinguishes predictive coding from standard backpropagation in terms of biological plausibility: backpropagation requires that backward-pass error signals be carried by the exact transpose of the forward-pass weight matrix (the weight transport problem ) and that a single global scalar loss be defined at a designated output layer–neither of which has a known biological implementation 48 . Predictive coding sidesteps both constraints because each node carries its own loss and gradient computation requires only message-passing between directly connected populations. Therefore, a particularly unifying formal move is to treat the brain’s generative model as a factor graph (or equivalent graphical representation) and view cortical computation as message passing that updates local sufficient statistics (beliefs) until global consistency constraints are satisfied. In this view, predictive coding corresponds to one important regime of variational message passing (especially under Gaussian/Laplace assumptions), while belief propagation / Bethe-style approximations become relevant for more discrete or structured latent variables (one discrete belief propagation formulation, Bio-RCN, is covered in the "Laminar Error Signals" subsection of "Cortical Error Signals") 45 , 50 . This reframes "prediction units" and "error units" as an algorithmic partition of messages rather than as a commitment to a particular anatomical ladder. The deepest invariance across these formalisms is not "hierarchy," but local computability . Regardless of whether the global dependency structure is a chain, a tree, or a loopy graph, the cortical algorithm remains: (i) maintain local beliefs about latent causes; (ii) receive local "context" messages from connected populations; (iii) compute local mismatches; (iv) update activity quickly and weights slowly to reduce a shared variational objective. This is why predictive frameworks remain plausible in a cortex whose wiring departs strongly from a feedforward stack. The resulting intuition that each neuron/column has its own "cognitive light cone" 51 can be made precise with Markov blankets : for any subsystem (a neuron, microcircuit, column, area, or network), a Markov blanket is a statistical boundary–sensory and active states–that renders internal states conditionally independent of external states given the blanket. In cortical terms, a neuron’s (or column’s) effective world is the set of variables that can influence it through its blanket; everything outside is "seen" only indirectly, via messages mediated by intermediate blankets. Importantly, a unit’s blanket-defined lightcone is not restricted to other cortical populations: it can encompass thalamic relay and modulatory inputs, basal-ganglia and cerebellar return projections, hippocampal signals, interoceptive and proprioceptive afferents, and diffuse neuromodulatory tone. "Prediction error" at a cortical node can therefore reflect mismatches with motor efference copies, expected reward-prediction errors, visceral states, or mnemonic reactivations–not only sensory reconstruction 2 , 3 . This dissolves the apparent boundary between perception and action: a motor-cortical neuron predicting proprioceptive reafference, a prefrontal neuron predicting the temporal pattern of dopaminergic reward-prediction errors, and a V1 neuron predicting retinal input are all performing the same operation–local free-energy minimization–over different cognitive lightcones. Markov blankets can be nested across scales (neurons ⊂ \subset columns ⊂ \subset areas ⊂ \subset networks), providing a principled way to talk about multiscale modularity without assuming a single global hierarchy 52 . A similar perspective is that the cortex contains many semi-autonomous predictive models operating in parallel (often anchored in different reference frames, modalities, or sensorimotor contingencies) that must be coordinated by long-range connections. On this view, cortex is not merely hierarchical but heterarchical : different subnetworks can be "higher" or "lower" depending on the variable being predicted, the timescale in play, and the current task demands 53 . Even within a single cortical area, lateral connections enable neighboring columns to predict each other’s activity–supporting intra-areal contextual phenomena such as surround suppression and contour integration 54 –so that "levels" of prediction exist not only across areas but also within them. This raises the question: if hierarchy is not a strict ladder, what are cortical "levels"? A robust operational definition is: a "level" corresponds to a set of latent variables sharing (i) a characteristic timescale (temporal receptive window / intrinsic timescale), (ii) a characteristic granularity (fine sensory causes vs coarse abstract causes), and (iii) a characteristic conditional dependency neighborhood (who sends/receives messages to whom). Under this definition, cortex supports multiple overlapping hierarchies (sensory, motor, interoceptive/visceromotor, social-cognitive, etc.) that interlock via shared factors and rich-club hubs, rather than a single canonical chain. In summary, the cortical predictive-coding framework does not require–and the anatomy does not support–a single chain-like hierarchy. The global free energy decomposes into a sum of local losses over nodes in a directed graph that may include lateral, skip, and recurrent edges; each node minimizes precision-weighted prediction error using only locally available signals, sidestepping the weight-transport and global-loss constraints that make backpropagation biologically implausible. The "cognitive lightcone" of any cortical unit–formalized via nested Markov blankets–extends beyond other cortical areas to encompass thalamic, subcortical, interoceptive, proprioceptive, and neuromodulatory afferents, so that prediction error can reflect mismatches with any structured regularity in a unit’s input, not only sensory reconstruction. "Levels" of the hierarchy are therefore best defined operationally–by shared timescale, representational granularity, and conditional-dependency neighborhood–yielding multiple overlapping hierarchies (sensory, motor, interoceptive, social-cognitive) interlocked via shared factors and rich-club hubs, rather than a single canonical ladder. Constraints and regularizers The above loss functions do not operate in a vacuum. Cortical learning unfolds within a biological substrate that imposes additional costs and hard constraints–some of which can be interpreted as implicit regularizers on the optimization, others as inviolable boundary conditions . Together, these shape the effective loss landscape in ways that go well beyond "minimize prediction error." We enumerate them here; the mechanisms by which cortex accommodates each are deferred to forthcoming sections (especially the "Cortical Plasticity" and "Plasticity" sections). The complexity cost is itself a regularizer. The KL-divergence term in Eq. 1– D K L [ q ϕ ( z | x ) | | p θ ( z ) ] D_{KL}[q_\phi(z|x)||p_\theta(z)] –already penalizes posteriors that deviate excessively from the prior, functioning as an intrinsic Occam’s razor that favors parsimonious explanations of sensory data 1 , 2 . This is not a separate constraint bolted onto the loss but a structural feature of variational inference: minimizing free energy inherently trades off accuracy against complexity. Nevertheless, the strength of this regularization is set by the precision of the prior, which is itself learned and modulated–meaning the effective weight of the complexity penalty is adaptive rather than fixed. Metabolic cost. The brain consumes roughly 20% of the body’s resting metabolic budget despite comprising  2% of body mass, and cortical spiking, synaptic transmission, and dendritic integration account for the bulk of this expenditure 55 . This relentless metabolic pressure acts as an implicit L 1 / L 0 L_1/L_0 -like penalty on neural activity and synaptic maintenance, favoring: Sparse, efficient coding. Representations in which only a small fraction of neurons are active at any time minimize metabolic cost per bit of transmitted information 56 , 57 . Sparsity also decorrelates population activity, reducing redundancy across units–linking metabolic pressure to classical efficient-coding and redundancy-reduction objectives 58 , 59 . Wiring-length minimization. Axonal and dendritic arbors are metabolically expensive to build and maintain. The observed compactness of cortical wiring–local clustering, topographic maps, the small-world architecture described above–can be understood as an optimization that minimizes total wiring cost subject to computational connectivity requirements 60 , 61 . Stability constraints. A system that updates billions of synapses in real time to reduce prediction error faces a constant threat of destabilization. Several interacting constraints enforce stability: Homeostatic regulation of firing rates. Cortical neurons must maintain their mean firing rates and population activity levels within a viable dynamic range; unchecked Hebbian potentiation would drive runaway excitation, while unchecked depression would silence circuits entirely 62 , 63 . Homeostatic plasticity mechanisms (synaptic scaling, intrinsic excitability adjustment, metaplasticity) function as implicit constraints that bound the operating point of the network. Excitatory/inhibitory (E/I) balance. Cortical networks operate in a regime of approximate balance between excitation and inhibition, which is critical for maintaining sensitivity to inputs, preventing epileptiform runaway, and supporting the irregular, asynchronous firing regime observed in vivo 64 , 65 . Perturbations to E/I balance must be corrected on timescales faster than or commensurate with learning-driven weight changes–imposing a constraint that shapes which synaptic updates are permissible at any moment. Synaptic normalization. Total synaptic strength onto a neuron cannot grow without bound; multiplicative or subtractive normalization of synaptic weights acts as an implicit constraint analogous to weight decay or norm penalties in machine learning 66 , 67 . Consolidation and catastrophic-interference avoidance. New learning must not overwrite previously consolidated representations that remain useful. This stability-plasticity tradeoff 68 imposes what amounts to a regularization toward previously learned weight configurations–implemented through mechanisms such as metaplasticity, synaptic tagging and capture, and sleep-dependent consolidation, discussed in later sections. Dale’s law. Each cortical neuron releases a single principal neurotransmitter (or co-transmitter complement) at all of its axonal terminals–excitatory neurons remain excitatory and inhibitory neurons remain inhibitory across all their postsynaptic targets 69 , 70 . This is a hard constraint, not a soft penalty: it partitions the weight matrix into sign-constrained submatrices and fundamentally shapes what credit-assignment solutions are reachable, distinguishing cortical learning from unconstrained artificial networks in which any weight can take any sign 71 . Biophysical stochasticity. Synaptic transmission is probabilistically unreliable (vesicle release probability at central synapses is often well below 1), ion-channel gating is stochastic, and spike generation is noisy–particularly in the low-firing-rate regime characteristic of cortex in vivo 70 , 72 . This irreducible stochasticity means that every gradient signal the cortex computes is corrupted by noise, effectively imposing a floor on the precision of any single synaptic update and favoring learning rules that are robust to high-variance gradient estimates–an implicit regularization analogous to the beneficial effects of stochastic gradient noise in machine learning 73 . Capacity constraints. The cortex, despite its vast scale, has a finite number of neurons, synapses, and dendritic compartments, and individual synaptic weights are stored with limited precision–estimated at roughly 4-5 bits per synapse based on spine-volume measurements 74 . These finite-capacity and finite-precision constraints force the system to compress high-dimensional sensory streams into lower-dimensional latent representations, favoring the extraction of abstract, generalizable statistical regularities over rote memorization of individual experiences. These constraints and costs do not merely limit cortical learning–they shape it, pushing the system toward sparse, balanced, stable, and metabolically efficient solutions that generalize well. How, precisely, cortex satisfies these constraints through specific plasticity mechanisms is the subject of the sections that follow. Relationship with superordinate objectives The cortical loss functions developed above–variational free energy and its temporal, active, and regularized extensions–specify the computational form of cortical optimization. But the content of that optimization–which prior preferences are installed, what counts as a "good" model, and what experiences are actively sought–is set by the superordinate objectives: intrinsic motivation , empowerment , and social control . Each maps onto a distinct aspect of the cortical loss landscape. Intrinsic motivation enters through both components of the free-energy objective. The complexity term D K L [ q ( z | x ) ∥ p ( z ) ] D_{\mathrm{KL}}[q(z|x)\|p(z)] is explicitly a coding cost–minimizing it implements the compression drive toward maximally compact, high-fidelity world models 75 , 1 . The metabolic and sparsity constraints tighten this compression further, penalizing the energy cost per bit of representation. The learning progress component–the drive to seek experiences that most rapidly improve the model–maps onto the epistemic value term in expected free energy: the expected reduction in posterior entropy 𝔼 q ( o τ | π ) [ D K L [ q ( s τ | o τ , π ) ∥ q ( s τ | π ) ] ] \mathbb{E}_{q(o_\tau|\pi)}[D_{\mathrm{KL}}[q(s_\tau|o_\tau,\pi)\|q(s_\tau|\pi)]] , which quantifies how much a predicted observation would tighten beliefs about hidden causes 42 , 76 . Policies maximizing epistemic value steer the organism toward state-space regions where its model is poorest and learning fastest–formally connecting Schmidhuber’s compression progress to the epistemic component of EFE 77 . The temporal hierarchy ensures this curiosity drive is itself hierarchically structured, with fast-timescale exploration nested within slower epistemic campaigns at higher cortical levels. Empowerment is supported by the sensorimotor structure of the cortical generative model. The action-conditioned state-transition function f θ ( z t , a t ) f_\theta(z_t, a_t) –present, per the APC framework, at every cortical level 4 –provides the forward model required to compute and maximize the channel capacity between actions and future states 78 , 79 . Within the EFE framework, empowerment emerges as a natural prior preference when the agent’s objective includes maintaining influence over its future: if prior preferences favor maximally diverse reachable states, policy selection approximates empowerment maximization 78 . The two APC error streams–state-transition prediction errors (refining the forward model) and action-prediction errors (refining the policy)–jointly optimize the agent’s model of its own controllability. Social control enters through prior preferences p ( o τ ) p(o_\tau) over social outcomes–being recognized, maintaining status, preserving relational bonds–whose violation generates high pragmatic prediction error within the EFE 80 , 81 . The cortical timescale hierarchy is directly relevant: the slowest dynamics in transmodal apex cortex (medial prefrontal, default-mode regions) encode the most temporally extended latent causes, including self-referential and mentalizing representations 22 , 25 . In the loss framework, these apex regions maintain slowly evolving priors about identity and social role–deep generative priors updated only by strong, sustained prediction error, lending the self-model its characteristic persistence. Social context further reshapes the effective loss via precision-weighting: social attention and social threat modulate the gain on prediction errors related to others’ evaluations and intentions, making them disproportionately influential on inference and learning 10 . At the highest levels, the generative model extends to include other agents’ beliefs and generative models, so that inter-agent prediction-error minimization–reducing divergence between one’s own model and the inferred models of significant others–becomes a formal expression of the drive toward mutual understanding 81 , 80 . In sum, variational free energy provides the mathematical form of the cortical loss; the three superordinate objectives determine its functional content . Intrinsic motivation is realized through the complexity term and the epistemic EFE component. Empowerment is realized through the action-conditioned generative model and the pragmatic EFE component (achieving controllable outcomes). Social control is realized through prior preferences over social observations, the slow self-model at the cortical apex, and the embedding of other minds within the generative hierarchy. The biological constraints–metabolic cost, sparsity, stability, Dale’s law, finite capacity–shape all three equally, delimiting which solutions cortex can reach. In this sense, each precision-weighted prediction error, at each level and timescale, is a local signal that–aggregated across the full spatiotemporal hierarchy–moves the system toward greater compression, greater controllability, and greater social coherence.

The laminar models reviewed above describe how error signals are computed and routed across populations of neurons organized into layers and thalamocortical loops. But populations are composed of individual neurons, each equipped with thousands to tens of thousands (e.g., ∼ \sim 5k–30k depending on layer/species) of synapses distributed across a morphologically complex dendritic tree, endowed with compartment-specific nonlinearities, and subject to distinct local plasticity rules at different dendritic locations. Understanding how error signals are computed at the single-neuron level is not merely a matter of finer resolution; it determines what comparison operations must be physically instantiated by dendritic and synaptic biophysics, what signals must be co-localized at each synapse for learning rules to approximate gradient descent on the global objective, and whether the brain requires separate "error neuron" populations or whether error computation can be folded into the dendritic machinery of ordinary pyramidal cells. Below, we examine several complementary proposals for how individual neurons participate in error-signal generation, beginning with the per-unit error computations implied by standard predictive coding, then advancing to multi-compartment dendritic models that exploit the pyramidal neuron’s biophysical architecture, and finally considering self-predictive, constraint-based, metabolic, and homeostatic signals that round out the effective error landscape at each cortical neuron. In canonical predictive coding, the per-neuron error computation falls directly from the global free-energy decomposition (under Laplace/Gaussian assumptions) described in "Cortical Loss Functions." Each "representation unit" j j at level l l –mapped onto deep-layer (L5/6) pyramidal cells in the Bastos et al. canonical microcircuit 1 –updates its activity μ j ( l ) \mu_j^{(l)} by integrating two error-driven signals: μ ̇ j ( l ) = − Π j ( l ) ε j ( l ) + ∑ k ∂ g k ( l − 1 ) ∂ μ j ( l ) Π k ( l − 1 ) ε k ( l − 1 ) , \begin{equation}
  \dot{\mu}_j^{(l)} \;=\; -\,\Pi_j^{(l)}\,\varepsilon_j^{(l)}
  \;+\; \sum_k \frac{\partial g_k^{(l-1)}}{\partial \mu_j^{(l)}}\;
  \Pi_k^{(l-1)}\,\varepsilon_k^{(l-1)},
\end{equation} where the first term is the precision-weighted prediction error at the neuron’s own level—arising because μ j ( l ) \mu_j^{(l)} deviates from the top-down prediction g j ( 𝝁 ( l + 1 ) ) g_j(\boldsymbol{\mu}^{(l+1)}) –and the second is the Jacobian-weighted, precision-scaled prediction error propagated from the level below, capturing the extent to which the bottom-up prediction errors at level l − 1 l{-}1 are attributable to μ j ( l ) \mu_j^{(l)} through the generative function g ( l − 1 ) g^{(l-1)} 2 , 3 , 4 . Importantly, both signals are locally available in principle: the top-down error arrives via feedback connections, while the bottom-up error arrives via feedforward connections–conforming to the laminar routing architecture described above. Each "error unit" at level l l –mapped onto superficial (L2/3) pyramidal cells–computes ε j ( l ) = μ j ( l ) − g j ( 𝝁 ( l + 1 ) ) \varepsilon_j^{(l)} = \mu_j^{(l)} - g_j(\boldsymbol{\mu}^{(l+1)}) ; its output is the error signal: a well-predicted state drives the error unit toward silence, whereas a poorly predicted state drives vigorous firing that propagates the mismatch upward through the hierarchy. The individual representation neuron therefore operates under dual error-driven pressure: it must simultaneously satisfy the top-down prior (reducing the prediction error imposed on it from above) and account for the evidence from below (reducing the prediction errors it causes at the next level down). This dual pressure–conform to expectations from above while explaining evidence from below–is the single-neuron signature of hierarchical Bayesian error correction. However, this formulation treats neurons as point units : each is a scalar value with a single input–output function and no internal spatial structure. For a point neuron, the error signals it sends and receives are scalar mismatches, and learning requires only that a Hebbian-like plasticity rule at the synapses implementing g l g_l is modulated by presynaptic activity and the postsynaptic error ε l \varepsilon_l 4 , 5 . This is the mathematical ideal. But cortical pyramidal neurons are not points; they are spatially extended, electrically compartmentalized structures with multiple semi-independent integration zones and a rich repertoire of active dendritic conductances (see "The Fundamentals" and 6 , 7 ). The central question for neuronal error computation is therefore whether the multi-compartment architecture of real pyramidal neurons can be exploited to implement richer error-signal generation than point-neuron predictive coding envisions— and, if so, whether these dendritic computations can subsume the role of dedicated error-neuron populations (which are required to compute ε \varepsilon in the 2012 "canonical" account), folding error computation into the internal structure of the very neurons that also carry predictions. A rapidly growing family of models answers this question affirmatively: the functional separation between basal/perisomatic and apical dendritic compartments of pyramidal neurons provides a biophysical substrate for computing per-neuron error signals—and, in several formulations, for approximating the error signals that backpropagation would require, using only locally available information 8 , 9 , 10 , 11 . The core idea, common to all such models, is deceptively simple. The basal and perisomatic dendrites, which receive primarily feedforward and local recurrent input, compute a "bottom-up drive"—what the neuron would produce given current input alone. The apical tuft dendrites, which receive primarily top-down feedback and contextual input in layer 1, convey a "teaching" or "target" signal—what the neuron should produce given higher-level context. The mismatch between these two compartmental voltages serves as a per-neuron error signal that can drive synaptic plasticity at feedforward synapses. Different models formalize this intuition with different biophysical and mathematical assumptions and arrive at different plasticity rules, but the underlying architectural motif is shared: the pyramidal neuron is a biological comparator , and the apical–basal voltage difference is the local error signal. An early and influential formalization of how compartmentalization enables local error computation was provided by Urbanczik and Senn 12 . Their model neuron has just two compartments—a dendritic compartment whose potential V w V_w is set by plastic synaptic weights, and a somatic compartment whose potential U U integrates dendritic drive with additional proximal ("nudging") input. The central construct is the dendritic prediction V w * V^*_w : the somatic potential that would obtain if all proximal input were silenced. They identify the key local error signal as the instantaneous spike-rate prediction error: S ( t ) − ϕ ( V w * ( t ) ) \begin{equation}
  S(t) - \phi(V^*_w(t))
\end{equation} where S ( t ) S(t) is the somatic spike train. This is the key conceptual move: the neuron’s own spikes become the teaching signal, and the dendrite’s predicted firing probability becomes the local “expectation”—so the comparator is intra-neuronal (dendrite ↔ \leftrightarrow soma), not a separate error population. This error enters synaptic change through their plasticity induction variable at each dendritic synapse i i : P I i ( t ) = ( S ( t ) − ϕ ( V w * ) ) h ( V w * ( t ) ) ∂ V 𝒘 ( t ) ∂ w i , \begin{equation}
  PI_i(t) = \bigl(S(t) - \phi(V^*_w)\bigr)\,h(V^*_w(t))\,
  \frac{\partial V_{\mathbf{w}}(t)}{\partial w_i},
\end{equation} where h ( V w * ( t ) ) = ϕ ′ ( V w * ) ϕ ( V w * ) h(V^*_w(t)) = \frac{\phi'(V^*_w)}{\phi(V^*_w)} is a positive gain factor that normalizes plasticity by the current predicted firing rate. Depending on the source of the direct input into the soma, the same dendritic error signal supports different learning regimes. They also make the unification claim explicit: if a reward signal modulates the learning rate, the same mechanism implements reinforcement learning—i.e., one compartmental prediction-error rule spanning supervised, unsupervised, and RL regimes. Urbanczik and Senn demonstrated that this single rule, without modification, could learn a supervised input–output mapping at the single-neuron level, support associative memory formation in a recurrent network (in which nudging patterns are delivered via somatic synapses during learning and subsequently recalled from dendritic input alone), and drive unsupervised self-organization of topographic maps (when the nudging arises from lateral recurrent connectivity within the network itself, so that the network computes its own teaching signal). Importantly, this model does not invoke the specific apical-basal morphology of pyramidal neurons—the dendritic compartment is generic, and the error signal arises from the separation between any dendritic predictor and the somatic output it attempts to forecast, rather than from a comparison between distinct input streams (e.g., bottom-up vs. top-down) arriving at morphologically segregated dendritic domains. This generality is a strength, not a limitation: by showing that compartmentalization alone —the minimal departure from the point-neuron assumption afforded by splitting a neuron into two electrically coupled but functionally distinguishable compartments—suffices for a biological neuron to compute a local error signal adequate for exact gradient-based credit assignment using only locally available quantities, Urbanczik and Senn established what is arguably the central theoretical result underlying the entire "dendritic error computation" research program. The subsequent models discussed below can be understood as instantiating this core principle—that the mismatch between what a dendritic compartment predicts and what the soma actually does constitutes an error signal sufficient for hierarchical credit assignment—in the specific anatomical configuration of cortical pyramidal cells, mapping feedforward and feedback streams onto the basal and apical dendritic compartments respectively and thereby connecting single-neuron error computation to error routing across cortical areas 9 , 10 , 11 . Sacramento, Costa, Bengio, and Senn proposed an influential a model in which error signals are encoded directly in the apical dendritic compartments of pyramidal neurons , eliminating the need for a separate population of dedicated error-coding cells and allowing the same neuron to simultaneously propagate activity (at the soma) and represent error (at the apical dendrite) 9 . The model builds on the dendritic predictive plasticity framework of Urbanczik and Senn, which derived a local synaptic learning rule from the principle that a dendritic compartment predicts the neuron’s somatic spiking output and updates its synapses to reduce the prediction error between somatic activity and dendritic expectation 12 . Sacramento et al. extend this single-neuron principle to a multilayer network by exploiting the three-compartment organization of neocortical pyramidal cells–basal dendrites, soma, and apical dendrites–as three functionally distinct integration zones that together implement a local error-computation circuit within each neuron. Each hidden-layer pyramidal neuron is modeled as a three-compartment unit. The basal compartment v B , k P v_{B,k}^P (P=pyramidal, B=basal, k=layer index) receives bottom-up (feedforward) input from pyramidal cells in the layer below via weights W k , k − 1 P W_{k,k-1}^P , encoding the neuron’s current prediction of its own activity based on ascending drive. The apical compartment v A , k P v_{A,k}^P integrates two opposing signals: (i) top-down feedback from pyramidal cells in the layer above via weights W k , k + 1 P P W_{k,k+1}^PP , and (ii) lateral inhibitory input from local SST-like interneurons via weights W k , k P I W_{k,k}PI . The soma u k P u_k^P integrates basal and apical contributions through conductance-based dynamics: d d t u k P = − g l k u k P + g B ( v B , k P − u k P ) + g A ( v A , k P − u k P ) , \begin{equation}
\frac{d}{dt}u_k^P \;=\; -g_{lk}\,u_k^P \;+\; g_B\,(v_{B,k}^P - u_k^P)\;+\; g_A\,(v_{A,k}^P - u_k^P),
\end{equation} where g B g_B and g A g_A are the effective transfer conductances coupling basal and apical compartments to the soma, respectively. This conductance-based formulation ensures that apical input acts as a modulatory influence on somatic output: when the apical compartment is at rest ( v A , k ≈ 0 v_{A,k}\approx0 ), the soma is driven primarily by the basal (feedforward) prediction; when apical voltage deviates from rest, it additively shifts somatic membrane potential, modulating the neuron’s firing rate. The apical compartment voltage is determined by the sum of top-down feedback and lateral inhibition: v A , k P = W k , k + 1 P P ϕ ( u k + 1 P ) + W k , k P I ϕ ( u k I ) \begin{equation}
  v_{A,k}^P=W_{k,k+1}^{PP}\phi(u_{k+1}^P)\;+\;W_{k,k}^{PI}\phi(u_k^I)
\end{equation} where ϕ \phi is the neuronal transfer function (a nonlinear function) and u k I u_k^I are the somatic potentials of the interneurons at the same layer. The key architectural insight is that this apical voltage will register a non-zero signal–an error–only when the top-down feedback from the layer above is not fully cancelled by the lateral interneurons. This transforms the apical dendrite from a passive recipient of feedback into an active error-encoding compartment. The lateral interneurons within each hidden layer serve a specific and critical function: they learn to predict–and thereby cancel–the top-down feedback that the network generates internally. Each interneuron is modeled as a two-compartment unit that receives lateral input from same-layer pyramidal cells on its dendrites (via weights W k , k I P W_{k,k}^{IP} ) and a weak "nudging" teaching signal from the corresponding pyramidal neuron in the layer above. Through plasticity at these lateral synapses (driven by the same dendritic prediction-error principle), interneurons learn to reproduce the activity of the upper-layer pyramidal population as a function of the current layer’s pyramidal activity–effectively learning the same input-output mapping as the feedforward pathway one level up. Once this mapping is acquired, the inhibitory projections from interneurons back to the apical dendrites of same-layer pyramidal cells ( W k , k P I W_{k,k}^{PI} ) learn to exactly negate the excitatory top-down feedback ( W k , k + 1 P P W_{k,k+1}^{PP} ), driving the apical compartment to its resting potential. Sacramento et al. term the resulting network state self-predicting : in the absence of any external teaching signal at the output layer, the lateral microcircuit fully explains (cancels) all internally generated top-down activity, rendering apical dendrites silent throughout the network. The self-predicting state is the equilibrium from which learning-relevant error signals emerge as deviations, and Sacramento et al. propose that its establishment could occur during a developmental period–consistent with the developmental maturation of excitatory-inhibitory balance observed experimentally 13 . The plasticity rule governing interneuron-to-pyramidal synapses W k , k P I W_{k,k}^{PI} has a particularly intuitive form: it drives the apical compartment voltage toward rest, Δ 𝑾 k , k P I ∝ ( 𝒗 r e s t − 𝒗 A , k P ) ( 𝒓 k I ) T \Delta \mathbf{W}_{k,k}^{\mathrm{PI}} \propto (\mathbf{v}_{\mathrm{rest}} - \mathbf{v}_{\mathrm{A},k}^{\mathrm{P}})\,(\mathbf{r}_{k}^{\mathrm{I}})^{T} –a dendritic variant of homeostatic inhibitory plasticity 14 that can be read as: "adjust inhibitory synapses until the apical dendrite is silenced." The computational power of the self-predicting state is revealed when a novel teaching signal is presented at the output layer–modeled as a somatic nudging current that pushes output-layer pyramidal neurons toward a desired target u N t r g t u_N^{trgt} . This nudging creates a discrepancy between the output the network would have produced on the basis of its feedforward weights alone and the target, shifting output-layer firing rates. Because the lateral interneurons have learned to predict only the self-generated top-down feedback (i.e., the feedback the network would produce without nudging), the now-altered top-down signal arriving at the apical dendrites of the penultimate hidden layer can no longer be fully cancelled. The residual–the mismatch between the nudged top-down feedback and the interneuron’s cancellation signal–appears as a non-zero apical voltage deflection 𝒗 A , k P ≠ 0 \mathbf{v}_{\mathrm{A},k}^{\mathrm{P}} \neq 0 . This deflection is the error signal. It propagates from the apical compartment down to the soma via the apical coupling conductance g A g_A , where it modulates the neuron’s firing rate and thereby induces plasticity at the basal (feedforward) synapses. Importantly, this error signal is neuron-specific: each pyramidal neuron receives a different top-down signal (weighted by its own column of 𝑾 k , k + 1 P P \mathbf{W}_{k,k+1}^{\mathrm{PP}} ) and has a different residual after interneuron cancellation, so the error is spatially resolved across the population–implementing unit-specific credit assignment without requiring a separate error-neuron population. All plastic synapses in the model obey a common form of dendritic predictive plasticity: d d t w = η ( ϕ ( u ) − ϕ ( v ) ) r , \frac{d}{dt}w = \eta\,\bigl(\phi(u) - \phi(v)\bigr)\,r, where u u and v v are somatic and dendritic compartment potentials, respectively, and r r is the presynaptic firing rate. For basal (feedforward) synapses on pyramidal neurons, the learning rule compares somatic firing rate ϕ ( 𝒖 k P ) \phi(\mathbf{u}_{k}^{\mathrm{P}}) against the rate predicted by the basal dendritic compartment alone, ϕ ( 𝒗 ̂ B , k P ) \phi(\hat{\mathbf{v}}_{\mathrm{B},k}^{\mathrm{P}}) (where 𝒗 ̂ B , k P \hat{\mathbf{v}}_{\mathrm{B},k}^{\mathrm{P}} is the basal potential attenuated by dendritic conductance ratios). In the self-predicting state without nudging, soma and basal prediction agree ( u k P = v ̂ B , k P u_k^P=\hat{v}_{\mathrm{B},k}^{\mathrm{P}} ), yielding zero plasticity induction–the network is at a fixed point. When a teaching signal is present, the apical error perturbs the soma away from the basal prediction, and the resulting discrepancy ϕ ( 𝒖 k P ) − ϕ ( 𝒗 ̂ B , k P ) \phi(\mathbf{u}_{k}^{\mathrm{P}}) - \phi(\hat{\mathbf{v}}_{\mathrm{B},k}^{\mathrm{P}}) ) drives weight updates in a direction that reduces the output-layer mismatch. The key biological virtue of this rule is that it depends only on quantities locally available at the synapse : the postsynaptic somatic firing rate, the local dendritic branch potential, and the presynaptic input–consistent with the phenomenology of voltage-dependent and STDP-like plasticity (covered in the next section "Cortical Plasticity") 15 , 16 . Sacramento et al. prove analytically that, in the weak-feedback limit ( λ → 0 \lambda\rightarrow0 , where λ \lambda is a parameter controlling the strength of top-down and teaching inputs relative to bottom-up drive), the synaptic weight updates prescribed by their dendritic plasticity rules converge to the weight changes computed by the backpropagation algorithm. Specifically, the somatic potential of a hidden-layer neuron decomposes into a feedforward term plus a backpropagated error term: 𝒖 k P = 𝒖 k − + λ N − k + 1 𝑾 k , k + 1 P P ( ∏ l = k + 1 N − 1 𝑫 l − 𝑾 l , l + 1 P P ) 𝑫 N − ( 𝒖 N t r g t − 𝒖 N − ) + 𝒪 ( λ N − k + 2 ) , \begin{equation}
  \mathbf{u}_{k}^{\mathrm{P}} = \mathbf{u}_{k}^{-} + \lambda^{N-k+1}\,\mathbf{W}_{k,k+1}^{\mathrm{PP}}\!\left(\prod_{l=k+1}^{N-1}\mathbf{D}_{l}^{-}\,\mathbf{W}_{l,l+1}^{\mathrm{PP}}\right)\mathbf{D}_{N}^{-}\!\left(\mathbf{u}_{N}^{\mathrm{trgt}} - \mathbf{u}_{N}^{-}\right) + \mathcal{O}(\lambda^{N-k+2}),
\end{equation} where u k − u_k^- is the activation of the purely feedforward network (obtained by removing all top-down and lateral connections), D l − D_l^- is a diagonal matrix of transfer-function derivatives evaluated at u l − u_l^- , and the product of 𝑫 l − 𝑾 l , l + 1 P P \mathbf{D}_{l}^{-}\,\mathbf{W}_{l,l+1}^{\mathrm{PP}} matrices is exactly the chain of Jacobians that backpropagation computes when propagating the output error ( u N t r g t − u N − u_N^{trgt}-u_N^- ) backward through the network. The resulting weight update for feedforward synapses from layer k−1 to layer k becomes: Δ 𝑾 k , k − 1 P P = η k , k − 1 λ N − k + 1 ( ∏ l = k N − 1 𝑫 l − 𝑾 l , l + 1 P P ) 𝑫 N − ( 𝒖 N t r g t − 𝒖 N − ) ( 𝒓 k − 1 − ) T + 𝒪 ( λ N − k + 2 ) , \begin{equation}
  \Delta\mathbf{W}_{k,k-1}^{\mathrm{PP}} = \eta_{k,k-1}\,\lambda^{N-k+1}\!\left(\prod_{l=k}^{N-1}\mathbf{D}_{l}^{-}\,\mathbf{W}_{l,l+1}^{\mathrm{PP}}\right)\mathbf{D}_{N}^{-}\!\left(\mathbf{u}_{N}^{\mathrm{trgt}} - \mathbf{u}_{N}^{-}\right)\!\left(\mathbf{r}_{k-1}^{-}\right)^{T} + \mathcal{O}(\lambda^{N-k+2}),
\end{equation} which, up to the scalar factor λ N − k \lambda^{N-k} (absorbable into the learning rate), equals the backpropagation weight change–provided the top-down weights equal the transpose of the corresponding feedforward weights. This weight-symmetry requirement is, of course, the classical weight-transport problem. Sacramento et al. address it in two complementary ways: (i) when top-down weights are set randomly and held fixed, the model implements feedback alignment 17 , in which forward weights align over learning to make use of the fixed random feedback; (ii) when top-down weights are made plastic according to a local rule that minimizes an inverse reconstruction loss, the model implements a form of target propagation 18 , 19 , in which the feedback pathway learns to approximately invert the forward pathway. In either case, exact weight symmetry is not required for successful learning in practice. A distinctive feature of the Sacramento et al. model relative to prior biologically oriented deep-learning proposals is that it operates continuously in time with all plasticity rules always active–there is no need for alternating between a "free" phase (network running freely) and a "clamped" phase (target imposed), as required by contrastive Hebbian learning 20 and equilibrium propagation 21 , or between distinct temporal phases in which apical activity is compared with and without the teaching input 10 . Instead, the simultaneous presence of top-down feedback (carrying the teaching-induced perturbation) and lateral inhibition (carrying the self-prediction that should cancel the unperturbed feedback) at the apical dendrite provides a spatial rather than temporal difference: the error is represented as a concurrent voltage deflection in one compartment, not as a subtraction across two time windows. This spatial encoding of error is continuously available to drive plasticity at all feedforward synapses simultaneously, making the model compatible with the brain’s apparent lack of globally synchronized learning phases. In their simulations, Sacramento et al. demonstrate that the model can learn a nonlinear regression task (approximating a random multilayer teacher network) and classify MNIST handwritten digits (achieving 1.96% test error with a 784-500-500-10-neuron network architecture)–performance comparable to feedback alignment and target propagation baselines (~1.6-2.4%) and substantially better than a shallow learner that updates only the output-layer weights (8.4%), confirming that the dendritic error signals propagate useful credit-assignment information to deep hidden layers. The Sacramento et al. model resonates with several architectural motifs identified in the preceding laminar-error-signal account, while also introducing distinctive commitments. The use of SST-like interneurons targeting apical dendrites to provide a cancellation signal aligns closely with the Bastos et al. canonical microcircuit’s assignment of SST + ^+ cells to the inhibitory component of the top-down prediction 1 , and with the empirical observation that prediction errors in mouse visual cortex arise from a failure to locally inhibit feedback 22 , 23 . The model’s three-compartment pyramidal architecture directly exploits the same dendritic compartmentalization–basal for feedforward drive, apical for feedback/contextual input–that undergirds the apical-dendritic precision-gating mechanism detailed above; the apical Ca 2 + ^{2+} electrogenesis reviewed in that context provides a biophysical substrate by which the weak conductance-based apical-to-soma coupling assumed in the model ( g A g_A ) could be amplified or gated in a state-dependent manner, linking the model’s error propagation to the precision-weighting architecture. There is also a notable alignment with the predictive routing framework 24 : in both, "error" is not computed by a dedicated subtractor neuron but emerges from the differential effect of top-down signals on pyramidal activity–in predictive routing via oscillatory-inhibitory preparation of specific feedforward channels, in Sacramento et al. via the homeostatic interneuron cancellation of self-predicted feedback. However, Sacramento et al. depart from canonical predictive coding in an important respect: whereas Bastos et al. assign superficial pyramidal cells (L2/3) the role of error units whose firing rate encodes ε l \varepsilon_l , Sacramento et al. encode error in the apical dendritic voltage of pyramidal neurons that also carry the representational signal at their soma. This means the same neuron simultaneously represents the posterior expectation (somatic rate) and the error (apical voltage), collapsing the two-population architecture of canonical predictive coding into a single multi-compartment cell. Whether this dual-use encoding is realized biologically–or whether cortex maintains at least a partial segregation into error-dominant and representation-dominant subpopulations–remains an open empirical question, but the dendritic model demonstrates that such segregation is not computationally necessary for backprop-approximate credit assignment, provided that the neuron’s morphological complexity is taken into account. The models reviewed above represent the apical error signal as a graded voltage deflection —a continuous, analog mismatch that weakly modulates somatic firing rate through a linear conductance coupling g A g_A . But cortical pyramidal neurons, particularly the thick-tufted L5 cells central to corticothalamic and corticofugal output, possess regenerative dendritic conductances that can transform such graded mismatches into discrete, all-or-none events: dendritic calcium spikes and plateau potentials . These events—and the somatic burst firing they trigger—constitute a qualitatively different error-signaling regime: high-amplitude, temporally extended, and sharply distinguished from background single-spike activity. The biophysical substrate is the BAC (backpropagation-activated calcium) firing mechanism 7 : when a backpropagating somatic action potential coincides with sufficient depolarization at the apical calcium spike initiation zone (at the main apical trunk bifurcation), a regenerative calcium spike is triggered that propagates to the soma and elicits a high-frequency burst of action potentials. Importantly, this requires the conjunctive presence of bottom-up drive (sufficient for a somatic spike) and top-down input (sufficient to depolarize the apical tuft)–precisely the conjunction that, in the Sacramento et al. framework, corresponds to a non-zero apical compartment voltage following incomplete interneuron cancellation of feedback. The burst/singlet distinction maps directly onto the error-signal framework: in the self-predicting state, apical dendrites are near rest and neurons fire isolated spikes driven by feedforward input alone—the "no error" regime. When a teaching signal or prediction violation creates an uncompensated apical depolarization that exceeds the calcium spike threshold, the resulting dendritic spike triggers burst firing—the "error present" regime. This discrete transition offers several advantages over graded voltage encoding as an error signal. First, high signal-to-noise ratio : dendritic calcium spikes produce strong depolarizations and drive somatic bursts with short (<10ms) inter-spike intervals that are readily distinguishable from single spikes by downstream neurons, providing a robust signal that does not require fine rate discrimination. Second, temporal extension : dendritic plateau potentials persist for ∼ \sim 50-200+ms–an order of magnitude longer than axonal spikes–generating sustained, compartment-specific calcium transients that can engage the calcium-dependent enzymatic cascades underlying synaptic modification (detailed in "Cortical Plasticity"). As Burger, Rule, and O’Leary demonstrate, this extended timescale also confers robustness to timing jitter : dendritic plateau potentials enable reliable coincidence detection even when afferent spikes arrive with the millisecond-scale temporal variability characteristic of cortical networks, ensuring that the error signal is not degraded by the asynchrony inherent in biological spike trains 25 . Third, nonlinear thresholding : the regenerative nature of the calcium spike imposes a sharp activation threshold, so that weak apical mismatches produce only subthreshold graded modulation while strong mismatches trigger the full burst response–effectively implementing a form of precision-dependent error gating , in which only sufficiently confident error signals elicit the discrete burst event that commands synaptic plasticity. This thresholding has a natural Bayesian interpretation: low-precision (small, uncertain) prediction errors are filtered out at the dendritic level, while high-precision (large, reliable) errors are amplified into unambiguous instructive signals. Leugering, Nieters, and Pipa further show that the interaction of localized plateau potentials across functionally compartmentalized dendritic branches enables neurons to integrate spike sequences across behavioral timescales far exceeding passive membrane time constants, suggesting that dendritic nonlinearities can sustain error representations across the temporal gaps that separate predictions from their sensory confirmation 26 . The relationship between dendritic spike-based error signaling and the graded-voltage models discussed above is one of refinement and amplification, not contradiction. Sacramento et al.’s linear apical-to-soma coupling can be understood as a first-order approximation to a system that, in biological neurons, includes a nonlinear amplification stage: subthreshold apical input produces the weak, continuous rate modulation the model assumes, while suprathreshold input engages regenerative calcium electrogenesis and triggers the discrete burst regime. The apical amplification mechanism and its thalamic/neuromodulatory gating discussed in the laminar-error-signals section ("Apical-dendritic Ca 2 + ^{2+} electrogenesis") provides the biophysical means by which this threshold can be dynamically adjusted—raising it in conditions of high uncertainty (suppressing error propagation when feedback is unreliable) and lowering it during states of high arousal or attention (facilitating error-driven learning when feedback is informative). In this view, the graded and discrete error-signaling modes are not alternatives but two regimes of a single dendritic mechanism , with the calcium spike threshold serving as a controllable gate that determines when an apical mismatch is sufficiently salient to be broadcast as a burst-encoded error signal to downstream plasticity machinery and postsynaptic targets. The instructive error signals reviewed above–dendritic apical-basal voltage mismatches, burst-encoded calcium-spike errors–convey specific information about how a neuron’s output should change to reduce a global task or inference objective. But cortical neurons are simultaneously subject to intrinsic error signals that arise not from mismatches between neural output and an external reference, but from deviations of the neuron’s own operational state from internally maintained set points. The most extensively characterized is the firing-rate homeostatic error : the discrepancy between a neuron’s time-averaged activity ⟨ r ⟩ τ \langle r \rangle_\tau and a cell-type-specific target rate r * r^* . Intracellular calcium concentration provides the biophysical readout, integrating spike-driven calcium influx over timescales of hours to days; calcium-sensing molecular cascades then transduce the mismatch into bidirectional adjustments of synaptic strength and intrinsic excitability 27 , 28 . From a loss-function perspective, this machinery performs approximate gradient descent on a homeostatic penalty ℒ h o m e o ∝ ( ⟨ r ⟩ τ − r * ) 2 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{homeo}} \propto (\langle r \rangle_\tau - r^*)^2 . Notably, the same ionic species–calcium–serves as the error currency for both the fast instructive signal (dendritic calcium spikes encoding apical mismatch on sub-second timescales, as reviewed above) and the slow homeostatic signal (time-averaged calcium concentration tracking mean rate over hours), with the two error channels distinguished by timescale and subcellular compartment rather than by molecular identity. The specific mechanisms by which homeostatic error reshapes synapses–synaptic scaling, metaplastic threshold shifts, intrinsic excitability regulation–are deferred to "Cortical Plasticity"; the essential point here is that homeostatic error constitutes an always-on background signal at every cortical neuron, constraining the operating regime within which instructive learning occurs and ensuring that weight modifications driven by task-relevant errors do not push neurons into pathological silence or saturation where gradient information is lost. A more dynamic generalization of this homeostatic principle is developed by Luczak and colleagues in the "neurons learn by predicting future activity" framework 29 . Rather than comparing current activity against a fixed set point, each neuron is posited to predict its own future activity–its firing pattern at the next time step or over a short temporal horizon–on the basis of its current pattern of synaptic inputs. The local error signal then becomes a temporal self-prediction error : the discrepancy between what the neuron predicted it would do and what it actually does. This objective is entirely local (depending only on quantities available at the single neuron) and unsupervised (requiring no external teaching signal or top-down feedback). Luczak et al. demonstrate that this principle, applied uniformly across a network, accounts for a range of empirically observed plasticity phenomena–including stimulus selectivity, temporal sequence learning, and response normalization–and interpret it as a single-neuron temporal instantiation of predictive processing: just as the cortical hierarchy minimizes prediction error across levels, each neuron minimizes the prediction error of its own future state. Classical homeostatic regulation then emerges as a limiting case: when the predicted future activity is time-invariant, the self-predictive error reduces to comparison against a constant target rate. When the prediction is input-dependent and temporally structured, the error signal captures stimulus-specific and sequential regularities beyond mean-rate deviation, blurring the boundary between homeostatic maintenance and representation learning. This self-predictive temporal error coexists with, and complements, the instructive dendritic errors reviewed above: the apical-basal voltage mismatch conveys what higher-level context demands of the neuron (an externally imposed error), while the self-predictive signal reflects the neuron’s own temporal model of its input statistics (an internally generated error). A related "prospective" minimization principle appears in the neuronal least-action framework of Senn et al., where pyramidal neuron voltage dynamics are posited to prospectively minimize the local somato-dendritic mismatch error within each neuron–implementing a form of anticipatory error reduction that bridges instructive and self-predictive objectives 30 . Metabolic constraints impose a final class of per-neuron error signal. Cortical computation is energetically costly–action potentials, synaptic vesicle cycling, and ionic-gradient maintenance claim a disproportionate share of the brain’s ∼ \sim 20W power budget 31 –and neural codes show pervasive evidence of pressure to minimize metabolic expenditure for a given level of information transmission 32 . At the single-neuron level, this pressure is captured by a metabolic penalty ℒ m e t a b o l i c ∝ c ( r i ) \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{metabolic}} \propto c(r_i) that increases with firing rate, favoring sparse, low-rate codes and serving as the biological counterpart of the L1/L2 regularization terms that prevent overfitting in artificial networks. Biophysically, metabolic state is read out through local molecular sensors–ATP/ADP ratio, extracellular adenosine released in proportion to synaptic activity, and astrocyte-neuron lactate shuttle dynamics–that modulate both synaptic efficacy and intrinsic excitability. Together, homeostatic set-point errors, self-predictive temporal errors, and metabolic costs define a background error landscape that each cortical neuron inhabits independently of any task-specific teaching signal. The effective per-neuron loss can therefore be understood as a composite: an instructive component (dendritic mismatch) driving credit assignment across levels, a homeostatic component (rate deviation from set point) preventing destabilization, a self-predictive component (temporal prediction error) promoting efficient encoding of input statistics, and a metabolic component (activity cost) enforcing representational parsimony. The interplay among these error channels–particularly how homeostatic and metabolic state modulate the gain, threshold, and direction of instructive plasticity through metaplastic mechanisms–is taken up in "Cortical Plasticity." The neuron-level proposals reviewed above converge on a central insight: the morphological complexity of cortical pyramidal neurons is not an obstacle to principled credit assignment but the substrate that enables it. The logical progression runs from canonical predictive coding’s point-neuron formulation–which requires dedicated error-coding populations (L2/3) separate from representation-carrying populations (L5/6)–through Urbanczik and Senn’s demonstration that splitting a neuron into just two electrically coupled compartments suffices for local gradient computation, to Sacramento et al.’s three-compartment model in which error is encoded in the apical dendritic voltage of the same neuron whose soma carries the representational signal. Active dendritic conductances then refine this graded mismatch into discrete, high-SNR burst-encoded errors, with the calcium-spike threshold providing a biophysical precision gate. Alongside these instructive signals, each neuron maintains a background error landscape–homeostatic rate errors, self-predictive temporal errors, metabolic cost signals–that constrains the operating regime within which task-driven learning occurs, mirroring at the single-cell level the multi-objective loss described in "Cortical Loss Functions." These neuronal mechanisms clarify, rather than compete with, the laminar proposals of the preceding subsection. The laminar models specify what populations must exchange what signals across what pathways; the dendritic models specify how individual neurons within those populations physically perform the requisite comparisons. The Sacramento et al. model’s SST-like interneuron cancellation at apical dendrites provides a single-neuron mechanism for the laminar-level operation that Bastos et al. assign to SST + ^+ cells and that predictive routing describes as pathway-selective inhibition: in each case, error emerges from the failure of an inhibitory signal to fully cancel descending input. The burst/singlet distinction at the dendritic level maps onto the predicted-vs.-unpredicted dichotomy at the columnar level–the Thousand Brains framework’s sparse-vs.-bursting responses, predictive routing’s differential gamma for unexpected stimuli, Sacramento et al.’s non-zero apical voltage after incomplete cancellation are all manifestations of the same principle at different scales. And if error is encoded in the apical compartment and prediction in the somatic rate of the same neuron, then the laminar-level disagreement over whether L2/3 “is” an error or prediction population is partly resolved: it depends on which readout–dendritic voltage or somatic firing–a given downstream target accesses. A remaining concern is that the dendritic credit-assignment models operate within chain-like architectures, whereas cortex is a dense, recurrent, skip-connected graph. As discussed in "From Hierarchical Levels to Heterarchical Graphs," Millidge, Tschantz, and Buckley close this gap by proving that predictive coding on an arbitrary computation graph 𝒢 = { 𝕍 , 𝔼 } \mathcal{G} = \{\mathbb{V}, \mathbb{E}\} converges at equilibrium to exact backpropagation gradients–achieved through simultaneous local relaxation rather than sequential backward sweeps, with no weight transport and no designated output layer. The local dendritic comparisons reviewed here therefore do not require strict feedforward organization to perform effective credit assignment. Yet "approximating backprop" remains a lossy abstraction of what cortex actually does: backpropagation presupposes a fixed graph, a single scalar loss, and synchronous phases–none of which hold biologically. The cortex’s computation graph is continuously reconfigured by attention and neuromodulatory state; its loss is distributed, precision-weighted, and temporally deep; and its populations update asynchronously. What the Millidge et al. result establishes is not that cortex implements backprop, but that local prediction-error minimization iterated across an arbitrary graph suffices to recover the gradients backprop computes–and this sufficiency is topology-independent. Backprop is best understood not as the algorithm the brain runs but as a normative upper bound on credit-assignment quality that local, biologically plausible relaxation dynamics can approach on exactly the kind of heterarchical graphs that brains are. The deepest invariant across these proposals is therefore architectural rather than algorithmic: at every synapse in the cortical sheet, the information needed to evaluate local consistency with the global objective is made available by the convergence of feedforward, feedback, and lateral signals onto distinct dendritic compartments–and the mismatch between compartments, read out through calcium- and voltage-dependent mechanisms, provides the error signal that drives learning.

The preceding subsections established the machinery by which cortical circuits compute self-supervised prediction errors (laminar and neuronal) and dynamically weight them according to inferred reliability (precision mechanisms). In the precision-weighting account, dopamine was characterized primarily as a gain modulator–setting effective precision Π l \Pi_l on prior and policy representations rather than carrying an instructive teaching signal per se. This characterization captures an important dimension of dopaminergic function at cortical targets, but it is incomplete in two respects. First, at cortical synapses, phasic dopamine (and, to varying degrees, other neuromodulators) also functions as a reinforcement-learning (RL) teaching signal that gates the consolidation of local Hebbian coincidences into lasting weight changes via three-factor plasticity rules–the "neuromodulator-gated (3-factor)" update rules listed in Table [tab:optimizers] for cortex and elaborated on in "The Fundamentals" and the upcoming section, "Cortical Plasticity" 1 , 2 . Second, the cortex itself generates a rich repertoire of reward- and outcome-related error signals–computed within cortical circuits by cortical neurons–that are distinct from both the self-supervised prediction errors of the laminar hierarchy and the phasic dopaminergic reward prediction errors (RPEs) classically associated with the basal ganglia. This subsection examines RL-related error signals as they are generated within and act upon cortical circuits, completing the cortical error-signal inventory before turning to the plasticity mechanisms that translate these signals into weight changes. The full treatment of the basal ganglia RL optimizer is deferred to its dedicated section ("The Basal Ganglia: Reinforcement Learning"); the molecular and biophysical details of three-factor plasticity are deferred to "Cortical Plasticity" and the "Synaptic Plasticity" subsection of "The Fundamentals". Cortex does not merely receive RL error signals from subcortical sources; it computes its own error signals related to reward, outcome, and action evaluation—signals that operate alongside, and in some cases upstream of, the midbrain dopaminergic RPE system. The best-characterized cortical locus for outcome-related error computation is the medial frontal cortex, particularly the dorsal anterior cingulate cortex (dACC) and adjacent medial prefrontal cortex (mPFC). Single-neuron recordings in macaque dACC have revealed cells whose firing rates scale with the magnitude of reward prediction errors—including, critically, both positive and negative surprises—producing unsigned outcome prediction errors that signal "how unexpected was this outcome?" rather than "was this outcome better or worse than expected?" 3 , 4 . These unsigned error signals are computationally significant because they can drive model updating and attentional reorientation without requiring access to the signed RPE that indexes value: detecting that an outcome was surprising, regardless of valence, is sufficient to signal that the current environmental model is inaccurate and should be revised 5 . Consistent with this, human neuroimaging evidence supports a role for dACC in tracking environmental volatility—the rate at which reward contingencies change—and using this estimate to adjust learning rates, a meta-learning computation that operates on outcome prediction errors rather than raw reward magnitudes 5 . Recent single-neuron recordings in both macaque and human medial frontal cortex have identified specific error-responsive neurons that are the likely generators of the error-related negativity (ERN) , a scalp-recorded event-related potential that peaks ∼ \sim 50-100ms after an erroneous response and is one of the most robust electrophysiological biomarkers of performance monitoring 6 , 7 . These error neurons respond to self-generated behavioral errors–a conjunction of intended action and actual outcome that constitutes a cortically computed action-outcome prediction error, generated within the medial frontal cortex’s own circuitry rather than being simply inherited from dopaminergic afferents; moreover, the discovery of homologous error neurons in macaque supplementary eye field and anterior cingulate cortex that are the direct generators of the ERN/error-related negativity provides a single-neuron-resolution bridge between the human scalp-recorded signal and the underlying cortical microcircuitry 8 . The relationship between these cortically generated error signals and the midbrain RPE has been debated: the influential RL–ERN theory proposed that the ERN reflects the impact of a phasic dopaminergic negative RPE on ACC 9 , but the single-neuron evidence now makes clear that ACC possesses its own error-computing machinery–neurons that fire to errors regardless of whether the error entails a reward loss, an incorrect motor response, or a violated temporal prediction–suggesting that ACC error signals are at least partly generated by a cortical action–outcome prediction mechanism rather than being passively inscribed by ascending dopamine 10 , but the single-neuron evidence now makes clear that ACC possesses its own error-computing machinery–neurons that fire to errors regardless of whether the error entails a reward loss, an incorrect motor response, or a violated temporal prediction—suggesting that ACC error signals are at least partly generated by a cortical action-outcome prediction mechanism rather than being passively inscribed by ascending dopamine 8 . The Alexander and Brown PRO (Predicted Response-Outcome) model formalizes this by proposing that mPFC learns to predict the probability and timing of each possible outcome given each possible action; surprise arises when an expected outcome fails to occur or an unexpected outcome occurs, generating both positive and negative outcome prediction errors that account for a wide range of ACC phenomena–including the ERN, the N2, feedback-related negativity, and reward positivity– within a single predictive-coding framework applied to the action-outcome domain 11 . A second cortical region contributing essential components to the RL error computation is the orbitofrontal cortex (OFC). Unlike dACC, OFC does not primarily encode outcome prediction errors per se; rather, it maintains detailed representations of expected outcomes —including their sensory-specific identity, magnitude, and probability—that serve as the predictions against which actual outcomes are compared by downstream evaluative circuits 12 . These representations are transmitted to midbrain dopamine neurons, where they become a critical input to the RPE computation. The causal evidence is direct: OFC lesions degrade the ability of dopamine neurons to signal expectancy-related changes in firing, reducing RPE signals as if the predictions required to compute them had been lost or degraded 13 . This positions OFC not as an error-computing area but as a prediction-generating area whose outputs are essential ingredients of the RL error signal—formally analogous to the role that deep-layer (L5/6) prediction-generating populations play in the self-supervised predictive-coding hierarchy, but specialized for the value and outcome domain. Similarly, recent intracranial recordings in humans during a probabilistic learning task have demonstrated that reward and punishment learning engage overlapping frontal cortical territories—including prefrontal and insular regions—but rely on dissociable inter-regional interaction patterns 14 . These cortically generated signals–unsigned outcome prediction errors in dACC, outcome predictions in OFC, volatility estimates that modulate learning rate, spectrally multiplexed reward and punishment signals across frontal cortex–converge on a critical cortical function: the cortex provides the belief-state representations and outcome predictions that make subcortical RPE computation possible under real-world conditions of ambiguity and partial observability . In environments where the hidden state must be inferred (partially observable tasks), a simple comparison of received reward against a cached expected value is insufficient; the agent must first infer which state it occupies, then compute the RPE conditional on that inferred state. This computation requires cortical input. Inactivation of rodent mPFC disrupts dopaminergic RPE signals specifically in tasks where the hidden state must be inferred from a history of observations, but not in tasks where the state is directly observable; computational modeling localizes this effect to a circuit in which mPFC furnishes the posterior belief over hidden states to the dopamine system, which uses this belief as the basis for computing the RPE 15 . Whole-brain viral tracing studies have confirmed that VTA dopamine neurons receive convergent monosynaptic and polysynaptic inputs from a wide array of cortical areas—including prefrontal, orbitofrontal, insular, and cingulate cortices—providing the anatomical substrate for this cortex-to-midbrain flow of state and value information 16 . The assembled picture is that the RPE signal broadcast by dopamine neurons is not computed in isolation by the midbrain but is a distributed computation to which cortex contributes the highest-dimensional inputs–inferred state, expected outcome identity, volatility-modulated learning rate–and from which cortex receives the compressed, low-bandwidth teaching signal in return. This cortex->midbrain->cortex teaching loop defines the channel through which RL error signals act back on cortical synapses. The return arm of the loop—phasic dopamine broadcast from VTA/SNc to cortex—is the third factor in the three-factor plasticity rule referenced in Table [tab:optimizers] and the "Synaptic Plasticity" section of "The Fundamentals". Importantly, this dopaminergic return projection is not spatially uniform across the cortical mantle. The density of dopaminergic innervation follows a pronounced gradient : frontal, premotor, and primary motor cortices receive the densest dopaminergic fiber plexuses; anterior cingulate, insular, and entorhinal cortices are moderately innervated; and posterior sensory cortices–including primary visual, auditory, and somatosensory areas–receive comparatively sparse projections (see next section "Cortical Plasticity" for greater detail) 17 , 18 . Within the heavily innervated frontal and motor territories, dopaminergic fibers further exhibit laminar specificity : in prefrontal cortex, the densest fiber plexuses occupy layers I-III and again layers V/VI–the same deep-layer compartment hosting the prediction-generating pyramidal neurons of the laminar hierarchy–while in primary motor and premotor cortex, a prominent projection targets layer I and the deep corticospinal output layers 19 , 20 . This innervation gradient is functionally interpretable through the RL teaching-signal framework developed above. Frontal and motor cortices sit at the policy-output end (or "decision-making" end) of the cortical hierarchy: prefrontal areas convert inferred goals and contextual beliefs into abstract action plans; premotor and motor areas convert those plans into concrete movement commands. These territories are precisely where reward-related teaching signals are most needed, because the core problem that RL must solve–credit assignment to the internal representations that generated rewarded or punished actions–requires that the teaching signal reach the synapses encoding the state-to-action mapping. Dense dopaminergic innervation concentrates the third factor at cortical sites where policy is computed and motor programs are specified , ensuring that eligibility traces at policy-relevant synapses have a high probability of encountering a phasic DA transient within their decay window τ e \tau_e . Posterior sensory areas, by contrast, can rely primarily on the self-supervised prediction errors of the laminar hierarchy for representational learning–their perceptual representations are shaped predominantly by the predictive-coding error signals described in the preceding subsections–consistent with their sparser dopaminergic innervation. Dopamine still plays a role in these sensory territories (e.g., attentional gain modulation, reward-modulated perceptual learning), but that role is subsidiary rather than dominant 21 , 22 . The heterogeneity of dopaminergic innervation thus determines not whether a cortical region can learn, but the relative weighting of self-supervised versus reinforcement-driven learning at each point in the hierarchy. At each cortical synapse, the local Hebbian coincidence between presynaptic activity and postsynaptic depolarization does not directly produce a lasting weight change; instead, it sets a transient eligibility trace e i j ( t ) e_{ij}(t) —a molecular flag, likely involving calcium/calmodulin-dependent kinase and related signaling cascades, which marks the synapse as a candidate for modification 2 . The trace decays exponentially with characteristic time constant τ e \tau_e unless a neuromodulatory signal (the "third factor") arrives within the eligibility window, at which point the trace is converted into a lasting change in synaptic efficacy. Schematically: e ̇ i j = − e i j τ e + f ( pre j , post i ) , Δ w i j ∝ e i j ( t ) ⋅ M ( t ) , \begin{equation}
\dot{e}_{ij} = -\frac{e_{ij}}{\tau_e} + f\bigl(\text{pre}_j, \text{post}_i\bigr), \qquad \Delta w_{ij} \propto e_{ij}(t) \cdot M(t),
\end{equation} where f ( pre j , post i ) f\bigl(\text{pre}_j, \text{post}_i\bigr) is an STDP-like function of pre- and postsynaptic spike timing (or voltage), and M ( t ) M(t) is the neuromodulatory signal–most commonly phasic dopamine for reward-related learning, but also norepinephrine (surprise/arousal), acetylcholine (attentional relevance), or serotonin (depending on receptor subtype and area) 1 , 2 . The critical parameter for cortical RL is the eligibility-trace time constant τ e \tau_e . In dorsal and ventral striatum, τ e \tau_e has been measured at ∼ \sim 0.3-1s 23 , 24 ; in visual and frontal cortex, eligibility traces for both LTP and LTD have been demonstrated with substantially longer time constants on the order of ∼ \sim 5-10s 25 , 2 , and hippocampal traces may extend to minutes 26 , 2 , 27 . These longer cortical trace durations are computationally significant: they extend the temporal window over which a delayed RL teaching signal can retroactively modify the synapses that were active at the time of a decision, bridging the gap between action and outcome characteristic of naturalistic behavior. The teaching role of phasic dopamine at cortical synapses is not in conflict with its precision-modulating role described above; the two functions are largely segregated by temporal dynamics and circuit locus. Tonic dopamine–the slowly fluctuating baseline concentration set by irregular, population-level VTA/SNc firing–modulates the gain and dynamic stability of cortical representations through sustained D1 and D2 receptor occupancy, implementing the precision function described in the "Neuromodulators as Precision-Weighting Machinery" subsection: setting attractor depth in PFC, biasing the exploitation-exploration trade-off, and tuning policy commitment (the γ \gamma parameter in the active-inference formalism) 28 , 29 . Phasic dopamine–the brief (~100-500ms), high-amplitude transient elicited by unexpected reward or reward-predicting cues–drives a rapid, spatially broad surge in DA concentration that acts on the eligibility-trace mechanism to convert recently flagged synaptic coincidences into durable weight changes. In active inference terms, tonic DA sets the precision (confidence) assigned to current policies, while phasic DA updates the generative model’s parameters–state-transition probabilities, outcome predictions–in light of new evidence about reward contingencies 30 . The distinction is not absolute: tonic and phasic signals are superimposed on the same axonal arbors and act on overlapping receptor populations, with functional outcomes depending on receptor affinity, local reuptake kinetics, and the recent history of DA release. But the separation by timescale is sufficient to support dual use of a single neuromodulatory channel for both precision control and teaching. This dual identity connects directly to the dendritic error-computation framework developed in "Neuronal Error Signals." Urbanczik and Senn explicitly demonstrated that their dendritic prediction-error rule—in which synaptic plasticity is driven by the mismatch between somatic spiking and dendritic prediction— subsumes RL as a special case : when the plasticity induction variable P I i ( t ) = ( S ( t ) − ϕ ( V w * ) ) h ( V w * ( t ) ) ∂ V 𝒘 ( t ) ∂ w i , \begin{equation}
  PI_i(t) = \bigl(S(t) - \phi(V^*_w)\bigr)\,h(V^*_w(t))\,
  \frac{\partial V_{\mathbf{w}}(t)}{\partial w_i},
\end{equation} is modulated by a scalar reward signal, the same compartmental comparator that implements self-supervised and supervised learning at a single neuron also implements a policy-gradient RL rule 31 . Sacramento et al.’s three-compartment extension preserves this property: the apical voltage deflection that encodes hierarchical credit-assignment error can be conjunctively gated by a neuromodulatory signal arriving at the same dendritic domain (e.g. via dopamine receptors expressed on apical dendrites of deep-layer pyramidal neurons), so that the direction of the weight change is set by the local dendritic mismatch and the magnitude (and whether it is consolidated at all) is scaled by reward-related feedback 32 . Viewed this way, the RL teaching signal does not introduce a fundamentally new error-computation circuit into cortex; rather, it modulates the gain and consolidation of the same dendritic mismatch signals that already drive self-supervised learning. Phasic DA acts as a multiplicative gate that converts "this synapse contributed to the current error" (the eligibility trace set by the dendritic comparator) into "this synapse contributed to the error and the outcome was better (or worse) than expected " (the consolidated weight change). The three-factor rule is therefore best understood not as a separate RL mechanism operating in parallel with the predictive-coding error architecture, but as a neuromodulatory elaboration of the same dendritic error-computation substrate—one that allows cortical plasticity to be shaped by outcome and reward signals without requiring the cortex to abandon its local, self-supervised learning logic and, ultimately, to consolidate state-action contingencies that improve expected outcomes under a preference distribution 33 , 34 . Alexander and Brown’s Hierarchical Error Representation (HER) model makes this integration fully explicit, demonstrating that a stack of cortical predictive-coding modules–each predicting the expected timing and identity of the next action-outcome contingency at a given level of abstraction–naturally gives rise to hierarchically organized error signals that encompass both sensory prediction errors (the domain of self-supervised cortical learning) and action-outcome prediction errors (the domain of RL), without requiring fundamentally different computational principles at any level 35 . In the HER model, each level of the medial and lateral PFC hierarchy learns to predict the probability and timing of outcomes conditional on actions at that level of abstraction; prediction failures propagate as error signals that drive both belief updating (analogous to perceptual inference) and policy updating (analogous to RL). The model reproduces a wide array of empirical phenomena across both lateral PFC (rule encoding, task switching, error-likelihood effects) and medial PFC (ERN, feedback-related negativity, N2, reward positivity), suggesting that the cortical RL error signals observed in ACC and mPFC need not arise from a dedicated RL subsystem embedded within cortex but instead emerge naturally from the same predictive architecture applied to action–outcome contingencies rather than—or in addition to—sensory state transitions. This resonates with the active predictive coding (APC) framework discussed in "Laminar Error Signals": APC posits that every cortical area maintains both a state-transition network and a policy network, with action prediction errors operating alongside state prediction errors as canonical columnar error signals 36 . The HER model can be read as the RL-facing interpretation of this same principle: wherever cortex predicts the consequences of actions, it automatically generates action-outcome error signals that support reinforcement-like learning within the self-supervised architecture. In synthesis, the cortical error-signal repertoire extends beyond the self-supervised prediction errors and precision weights developed in the preceding subsections to encompass a family of RL-related signals: cortically generated unsigned outcome prediction errors (dACC/mPFC), expected-outcome representations that are necessary inputs to the subcortical RPE computation (OFC), reward and punishment error signals across frontal cortex, belief-state inferences that determine the content of the dopaminergic RPE under partial observability (mPFC->VTA), and the phasic dopaminergic teaching signal that returns to cortex to gate synaptic consolidation via eligibility traces. Moreover, the density of dopaminergic innervation is itself spatially graded—heaviest at the frontal and motor policy-output territories, lightest in posterior sensory cortex—so that the relative influence of RL-gated versus self-supervised plasticity shifts systematically across the cortical hierarchy. The key architectural insight—and the reason these signals belong in a section on cortical error signals rather than being deferred entirely to the basal ganglia—is that they do not constitute a separate "RL module" grafted onto the cortical sheet. Rather, they are deeply intertwined with the self-supervised predictive architecture: the same laminar circuits that generate sensory prediction errors also generate outcome prediction errors (via the same predictive logic applied to action–outcome contingencies); the same dendritic compartmentalization that implements local credit assignment also supports three-factor gating by neuromodulatory third factors; and the eligibility-trace mechanism bridges these two regimes by allowing delayed reward signals to retroactively sculpt synapses whose Hebbian coincidences were flagged by the fast, self-supervised error-computation machinery. The result is a cortex in which self-supervised and reinforcement error signals share a common computational substrate and are distinguished primarily by the timescale and source of the teaching signal—immediate local mismatch for self-supervised updating, delayed neuromodulatory broadcast for RL—rather than by fundamentally different circuitry. The mechanisms by which cortical synapses implement this shared substrate—the molecular identity of eligibility traces, the receptor cascades activated by each neuromodulator, and the metaplastic rules governing the interaction between self-supervised and RL-driven weight modifications—are the subject of the next section.

What, exactly, are the basal ganglia optimizing? In the most classical reinforcement-learning formulation, the basal ganglia optimize policies so as to maximize expected cumulative reward: they adjust the probability, timing, and vigor of actions according to whether those actions lead to better-than-expected or worse-than-expected outcomes. Thus, unlike the cortex, which minimizes precision-weighted prediction error over a high-dimensional generative model, the basal ganglia optimize a family of behaviorally consequential objectives defined over policy selection, policy execution, and policy timing. Their core problem is not to construct a richly structured world model, but to evaluate candidate actions defined over cortical state representations and to bias behavior toward those expected to improve future outcomes. Those “actions” should be understood broadly: not only overt motor outputs, but also covert cognitive and affective operations; value appraisals can be computed over inferred latent states, predictive state representations, and other cortex/hippocampus-supplied features [Dopamine reward prediction errors reflect hidden-state inference across time; What is dopamine doing in model-based reinforcement learning?]. In that sense, the basal ganglia are best understood as the brain’s principal policy optimizer, embedded in cortico-basal-ganglia-thalamo-cortical loops that channel limbic, associative, and sensorimotor information into competitive selection and dopaminergic credit assignment 1 , 2 , 3 . In active-inference language, one can say that cortex supplies much of the state space and predictive structure, whereas basal ganglia help convert preferences over outcomes into biases over policies, thereby helping steer both overt and covert action toward preferred states of the world 4 , 5 , 6 . The scalar RPE as the canonical BG loss To optimize a policy one needs a training signal that evaluates how well current action selection is doing. In the basal ganglia, that signal likely derives from the Bellman consistency condition , which defines the value of a state as the expected immediate reward plus the discounted value of its successor: V ( s ) = 𝔼 [ r + γ V ( s ′ ) ] \begin{equation}
  V(s)=\mathbb{E}[r+\gamma V(s')]
\end{equation} If the agent’s value estimates are correct, successive predictions should agree. The temporal-difference (TD) error δ = r + γ V ( s ′ ) − V ( s ) \begin{equation}
  \delta =r+\gamma V(s')-V(s)
\end{equation} measures the violation of this agreement at each time step 7 , 8 . Here, δ \delta does double duty. Minimizing 𝔼 [ δ 2 ] \mathbb{E}[\delta^2] trains the value function (the "critic"), but the sign and magnitude of δ \delta simultaneously provide the gradient information the system needs to adjust the policy (the "actor"): a positive δ \delta stamps in whichever action or context preceded it, a negative δ \delta stamps it out, and zero δ \delta leaves the current policy unchanged. It is this dual role that makes a single scalar error sufficient to drive policy optimization over arbitrarily large action spaces–the BG need not represent a full error vector over every possible action, but only broadcast whether the most recently selected action turned out better or worse than expected. A large body of evidence identifies the phasic activity of midbrain dopamine neurons with precisely this TD error signal. Dopamine neurons produce bursts following unexpected rewards, transfer their phasic response timing from the unconditioned stimulus to reward-predictive cues with learning, and pause at the time of expected but omitted rewards—each observation conforming quantitatively to the TD prediction error 8 , 9 , 10 , 11 . Causal manipulations (e.g. optogenetic activation or inhibition of dopamine neurons timed to reward events) produce behavioral changes consistent with the TD learning algorithm, confirming that δ \delta is not merely correlated with learning but plays a causal role in driving it 12 , 13 , 14 . The upshot is that the phasic dopamine broadcast constitutes a policy-relevant loss signal : it is the error term whose iterative minimization reshapes striatal action-selection weights toward an improved policy. Average-reward formulation and policy timing/vigor The discounted-return framing captures how basal ganglia learn which action to select, but a policy is defined not only by choice but also by timing, vigor, and threshold. In continuing, free-operant, or self-paced settings an average-reward formulation brings these additional policy dimensions into focus. Here the relevant baseline is not discounted future value but the long-run average reward rate ρ \rho , and the error term takes the differential form δ = r − ρ + V ( s ′ ) − V ( s ) \begin{equation}
  \delta =r-\rho+V(s')-V(s)
\end{equation} The policy-optimization interpretation is immediate: ρ \rho represents the opportunity cost of time . When average reward rate is high, every moment spent deliberating, waiting, or moving slowly is a moment that could have been spent collecting reward; the optimal policy therefore responds faster, moves more vigorously, and lowers its effective decision threshold. When ρ \rho is low, the opportunity cost of time drops and a more cautious, energy-conserving policy is preferred. In this way the average-reward formulation extends the BG’s policy-optimization mandate from the content of action (what to do) to its dynamics (when to do it, how hard, and at what evidence threshold), linking reinforcement-learning theory to literatures on vigor, latency, effort allocation, and urgency 15 , 16 , 17 . The average-reward account should nevertheless be stated with some care. It remains influential, and there is convergent evidence that dopaminergic function is related to reward-rate-dependent invigoration and to reduced decision thresholds during reinforcement learning, but the field has not converged on a simple one-to-one identification of tonic dopamine with ρ \rho . Recent human and animal work is more consistent with a broader picture in which dopamine contributes jointly to learning, action selection, movement vigor, and reward-rate-sensitive control, without forcing those roles into a single literal coding interpretation. A clean synthesis, then, is that the two formulations carve up the same policy-optimization problem along complementary axes: the discounted TD formulation best captures how the BG learn state and action values to determine the content of choice, whereas the average-reward formulation best captures how they regulate the temporal envelope—tempo, vigor, threshold—within which that choice is executed. These are most productively treated as complementary slices through a single effective loss landscape rather than as mutually exclusive theories 18 , 17 , 19 . Actor-critic models of the striatum In reinforcement learning, the actor-critic architecture factors the policy-optimization problem into two coupled subproblems: a critic that learns a state-value estimate V ( s ) V(s) , and an actor that learns a policy π ( a ∣ s ) \pi(a∣s) over actions 7 . Importantly, the critic’s value estimate is not an end in itself–it exists to enable computation of the TD error δ = r + γ V ( s ′ ) − V ( s ) \delta =r+\gamma V(s')-V(s) , which then serves as the training signal for both modules. The critic uses δ \delta to correct its own value estimates (minimizing 𝔼 [ δ 2 ] \mathbb{E}[\delta^2] ), while the actor uses the sign and magnitude of δ \delta to reinforce or suppress whichever action preceded the outcome (ascending the expected-return surface). The value estimate thus sits at the root of the entire optimization: if the critic is inaccurate, the TD error is noisy, and the actor’s policy updates are correspondingly degraded. The architecture is "shared-error, asymmetric-role": a single scalar δ \delta trains both modules, but it is the critic that makes δ \delta possible in the first place. A longstanding hypothesis maps this factored architecture onto striatal anatomy: the ventral striatum (particularly the nucleus accumbens) serves as the critic, and the dorsal striatum serves as the actor 20 , 1 . The proposed computational flow is as follows. Ventral striatal neurons learn state-value estimates through cortical, limbic, and thalamic afferents. Those value signals are communicated–via ventral striatal projections to the ventral pallidum and thence to midbrain dopamine nuclei–to the circuitry that computes δ \delta 21 , 22 . Dopamine neurons then broadcast the resulting TD error to both ventral and dorsal striatum simultaneously 23 . In ventral striatum, δ \delta drives critic learning–refining the value estimates from which future errors will be derived. In dorsal striatum, the same δ \delta drives actor learning–adjusting action-selection weights so that policies associated with positive surprise are reinforced and those associated with negative surprise are weakened. The ventral striatal value estimate therefore "trains" dorsal striatal policy not through a direct ventral-to-dorsal projection, but through its foundational role in shaping the dopaminergic error signal on which both systems depend. Several lines of evidence support this division of labor, if imperfectly. Action-preference and chosen-action signals are reliably found in dorsal striatum but are weak or absent in ventral striatum 24 , 25 , 26 , 27 , consistent with an actor role. Ventral striatal activity, conversely, tracks state value and reward expectation in a manner consistent with a critic 28 . And dopamine projections to both regions carry quantitatively similar TD error signals, as the architecture requires 23 . The dissociation sharpens under manipulations that isolate the two roles: ventral striatal responses appear whenever reward is predicted–under Pavlovian as well as instrumental contingencies–whereas dorsal striatal responses emerge specifically when an action must be learned to procure that reward 28 , precisely the double dissociation the partition predicts, since value prediction is demanded in both settings but policy learning only when behavior is instrumental. Lesion and inactivation work converges: disrupting dorsal–especially dorsolateral–striatum preferentially impairs the acquisition of stimulus-response habits while leaving reward prediction and outcome expectancy comparatively intact 29 , an actor-selective deficit. The mapping also has an anatomical substrate for the ventral-to-dorsal flow it presupposes: striato-nigro-striatal projections in the primate are organized as an ascending spiral, in which ventral striatal sectors influence midbrain dopamine populations that project back to slightly more dorsal territory, and so on in an open, feed-forward cascade from shell toward dorsolateral striatum 30 –exactly the directional, serial connectivity the critic-trains-actor story requires, with the value-carrying ventral sectors sitting upstream of the action-selecting dorsal sectors within the dopaminergic loop rather than the two being symmetrically coupled. The division is nonetheless imperfect in the ways one expects of a heuristic mapped onto biology. Action-value and chosen-value signals are not strictly confined to dorsal striatum–they appear, more weakly, in ventral territory in some tasks, and state-value signals are readily recorded dorsally–so the actor/critic boundary behaves as a gradient rather than a partition 26 . More fundamentally, whether the dopaminergic teaching signal is the pure state-value error δ \delta of a textbook actor-critic, or an action-value error closer to SARSA or Q-learning, remains unsettled: dopamine activity has been shown to track the value of the upcoming, about-to-be-chosen action rather than state value alone, which the strict critic-teaches-actor scheme does not straightforwardly predict 31 . The architecture is thus best read as a well-supported first-order description–critic ventral, actor dorsal, shared dopaminergic error–onto which the biology layers overlap, graded transitions, and an open question about exactly which error the system computes. From scalar to distributional: the BG loss is richer than a single mean The classical TD account treats the basal ganglia as learning a single expected return: the critic estimates the mean future value of a state or action, and phasic dopamine reports the scalar error relative to that mean. But when outcomes are stochastic, the mean is only one statistic of practical relevance. A policy may care not only about expected payoff, but also about dispersion, downside risk, upside potential, and the temporal profile with which rewards arrive. In distributional reinforcement learning , the learned object is therefore not just the scalar value V ( s , a ) = 𝔼 [ Z ( s , a ) ] V(s,a)=\mathbb{E}[Z(s,a)] , but the full return distribution Z ( s , a ) Z(s,a) itself. Computationally, this is achieved by maintaining a family of parallel value predictors with different asymmetric sensitivities to positive and negative prediction errors, such that different channels converge to different quantiles or expectiles of the outcome distribution rather than all collapsing onto the mean. From a loss-function perspective, this reframes the BG objective as something richer than minimizing a single mean-squared TD loss: the system instead minimizes a coordinated set of asymmetric regression losses whose collective solution approximates the full distribution of future returns 32 , 33 , 34 . This moved from an attractive AI analogy to a serious biological hypothesis when Dabney and colleagues showed that dopamine neurons are not perfectly redundant copies of one scalar RPE. Instead, individual dopamine neurons displayed stable heterogeneity in their reversal points (i.e where neurons shift from signaling above baseline for high-value rewards to below baseline for low-value rewards) and in the relative scaling of positive versus negative prediction errors, precisely the pattern expected if different channels were learning different expectiles of the reward distribution 33 . Decoding from this heterogeneous population recovered multimodal reward distributions that standard mean-based TD learning cannot represent. On this view, the dopaminergic teaching signal is still an RPE, but not a single homogeneous number in any literal circuit sense; it is better described as a structured population of distributional RPE channels, spanning more optimistic and more pessimistic forecasts of future reward 33 , 32 . More recent work has pushed this story downstream into the striatum itself. In a task that dissociated reward mean from reward variance, Lowet and colleagues found robust abstract encoding of variance in striatum, showed that chronic ablation of dopamine inputs disrupted these distributional representations without abolishing mean-value coding, and further found that D1 and D2 medium spiny neurons preferentially encoded the right and left tails of the reward distribution, respectively. That result is especially important for a basal-ganglia loss-function section, because it suggests that the direct and indirect pathways may participate in an opponent distributional code : not merely “Go” versus “NoGo” around a single scalar value, but optimistic versus pessimistic slices of the return distribution that can support sensitivity to uncertainty and risk. In that sense, the BG loss may be biologically implemented as a vector of partially opponent objectives over the distribution of possible outcomes, rather than as one scalar error around one scalar prediction 35 . That said, this should still be framed as a strong emerging account , not as settled biology. For instance, recent work shows that dopaminergic heterogeneity can also be explained by feature-specific prediction errors, where heterogeneity arises because different dopamine neurons receive different state-feature inputs rather than different asymmetric scaling factors 36 . The two frameworks are not mutually exclusive; they likely address different sources of heterogeneity (outcome-specific asymmetry versus input-specific projection topography), and a complete account of the BG loss landscape may require both 36 , 35 . In addition, changes in tonic dopamine levels can shift the balance of D1- versus D2-receptor sensitivity and thereby induce population-level optimistic or pessimistic biases in value learning–a mechanism complementary to the phasic-response asymmetries that underlie distributional coding at the single-neuron level 37 . What can be said with increasing confidence is that the BG loss signal, once idealized as a single scalar δ \delta , is better understood as a structured, heterogeneous population code whose individual components minimize different members of a family of asymmetric loss functions, and whose aggregate output supports both the expected-value computations required for standard policy optimization and the distributional computations required for a richer, more adaptive relationship with uncertainty. RL across timescales The frontier picture is richer still, because the relevant distribution appears to extend over time as well as reward magnitude. Classical TD learning compresses all future rewards into a single scalar value through one fixed discount factor γ \gamma , and therefore through a single temporal horizon: a single value of γ \gamma implies a single answer to the question "how far into the future should the agent care?" But animals must assign credit and select actions across an enormous range of timescales–from sub-second motor adjustments during a song or a reach to multi-hour foraging expeditions–and a single discount factor cannot accommodate this range without either myopically ignoring distant rewards or slowly blurring the distinction between cues that predict reward at different delays 7 , 38 . Multi-timescale RL proposes that the brain maintains a family of parallel TD learners, each operating with a different discount factor γ i \gamma_i , so that the dopaminergic teaching signal is not a single scalar δ \delta but a structured vector of prediction errors spanning short to long planning horizons 38 , 39 , 40 . At the level of dopamine release within the striatum , this idea receives striking anatomical support. Mohebi, Wei, and colleagues simultaneously recorded dopamine transients in the dorsolateral (DLS), dorsomedial (DMS), and ventral (VS) striatum of rats performing instrumental and Pavlovian tasks, and found that dopamine dynamics systematically accelerated from ventral to dorsomedial to dorsolateral striatum across three functionally distinct measures: the tempo of spontaneous dopamine fluctuations, the time constant over which prior reward history was integrated to form predictions, and the rate at which cue-predicted future rewards were discounted 41 . In a multiple-delay Pavlovian task, dopamine responses to cues predicting reward at 0.6, 3, and 12 seconds showed the steepest discount in DLS and the shallowest in VS, with DMS intermediate–and a recurrent neural network model with three pools of LSTM units at τ \tau =2s, 10s, and 1,000s reproduced the distinctive subregional response profiles, including weak DLS cue responses (because the cue-predicted reward is too temporally distant for a short-horizon learner), sluggish VS cue discrimination (because a long-horizon learner pools value across many trials), and sharp DMS discrimination (because its intermediate horizon best matches the trial-length timescale of the Pavlovian task) 41 . This topographic gradient maps naturally onto the known hierarchical organization of cortico-basal ganglia loops, in which ventromedial striatum participates in limbic circuits that organize behavior over long timescales, dorsomedial striatum in associative circuits for cognitive control, and dorsolateral striatum in sensorimotor circuits for rapid, habitual responding 42 , 43 , 44 . For the BG loss function, this gradient means that the effective discount factor γ \gamma entering the TD error δ = r + γ V ( s ′ ) − V ( s ) \delta =r+\gamma V(s')-V(s) is not a single system-wide constant, but varies topographically across striatal subregions, so that each cortico-basal ganglia loop effectively minimizes a different temporal slice of the Bellman consistency condition. The same logic applies at the level of individual midbrain dopamine neurons . Masset, Tano, and colleagues recorded optogenetically identified VTA dopamine neurons in mice performing a cued-delay reward task and a virtual-reality approach task, and found robust heterogeneity in the discount factor γ \gamma across individual neurons: some neurons responded strongly only to cues predicting the shortest delay, while others showed gradually decaying responses across much longer delays 39 . Single-neuron discount factors were best fit by exponential (not hyperbolic) decay functions, were stable across random trial splits, and were correlated across the two tasks , indicating that the temporal discount factor is a cell-specific property of individual dopamine neurons rather than a context-dependent parameter 39 . This diversity has a powerful computational consequence: by applying a regularized inverse Laplace transform to the population vector of cue responses (each weighted by the neuron’s fitted γ i \gamma_i ), Masset and colleagues could decode the probability distribution over future reward times from a single snapshot of the population response–a computation that is mathematically impossible from a single-timescale scalar value 39 , 34 . In simulations, agents equipped with multiple discount factors outperformed single-timescale agents at disentangling reward magnitude from delay, at converting between exponential and hyperbolic discount functions, and at inferring reward timing even before value learning had converged–capabilities that are ecologically essential but inaccessible to a homogeneous δ \delta 39 . Published simultaneously with the Masset et al. study, Sousa, Bujalski, and colleagues extended this framework to the joint distribution over reward time and magnitude . In their time-magnitude RL (TMRL) model, individual dopamine neurons are characterized by two heterogeneous parameters: a discount factor γ i \gamma_i (governing temporal sensitivity) and an asymmetric RPE scaling parameter κ i \kappa_i (governing sensitivity to reward magnitude, as in the distributional account discussed above). Combining these two axes of heterogeneity, the authors showed that just 450 ms of the dopamine population response to a reward-predictive cue was sufficient to decode a two-dimensional probabilistic "map" of future reward over both "when" and "how much" 45 . The decoded reward-time predictions correlated with trial-by-trial anticipatory licking, suggesting that this distributional temporal code is not merely epiphenomenal but is actually used to guide the timing of behavior 45 . Moreover, the TMRL model predicts–and the data confirm–that the distribution of discount factors across neurons adapts to reward statistics in accordance with efficient coding principles: when the longest-delay cue was removed from the task, neurons shifted their discount factors to devote more coding resolution to short delays, and vice versa 45 . For a BG loss-function account, the multi-timescale picture has three major implications. First, it means the BG loss landscape is not parameterized by a single γ \gamma but by a spectrum of discount factors distributed across both striatal subregions and individual dopamine neurons, so that the effective objective being optimized at any given level of the cortico-basal ganglia hierarchy is 𝔼 [ δ 2 ] \mathbb{E}[\delta^2] for a circuit-specific γ i \gamma_i . Second, when combined with the distributional account (asymmetric RPE scaling across neurons), it implies that the dopaminergic teaching signal carries not just the expected value of future reward but something approaching its full joint distribution over time and magnitude –a far richer error signal than the original scalar δ \delta , and one that can support risk-sensitive, time-sensitive, and state-dependent policy optimization 45 , 39 . Third, the observation that individual dopamine neurons carry stable, cell-intrinsic discount factors that correlate across tasks constrains the biological implementation: the spectrum of temporal horizons appears to be built into the midbrain itself, perhaps through molecular or circuit-level heterogeneity in dopamine neuron properties, rather than being flexibly reassigned trial by trial 39 . Taken together, the distributional and multi-timescale extensions do not displace the classical TD account of the BG loss but unfold it: what was once idealized as a single scalar error minimizing 𝔼 [ δ 2 ] \mathbb{E}[\delta^2] around a single discounted value is better understood as a structured population of errors, distributed over both reward-magnitude quantiles and temporal horizons, whose collective minimization shapes a policy that is sensitive not only to expected payoff but to its full probabilistic landscape in time. Territory-specific objective functions The preceding subsections traced the BG loss from its scalar TD origin through distributional and multi-timescale extensions; taken together, these analyses suggest that the dopaminergic teaching signal is far richer than a single number minimizing a single 𝔼 [ δ 2 ] \mathbb{E}[\delta^2] . The present subsection asks: what representations does δ \delta operate over, in which striatal territories, and how does this vary across the parallel cortico-basal ganglia loops? Answering this question is essential because the effective objective function optimized by any given BG circuit depends jointly on (i) the state representation feeding into the value estimate, (ii) the temporal horizon of the prediction, and (iii) the behavioral domain the loop controls. The "many worlds" hypothesis of dopamine prediction error captures this intuition: because the basal ganglia are organized as a set of parallel circuits, each receiving distinct cortical inputs and projecting to distinct downstream targets, the same formal TD error may carry qualitatively different information in different loops–different "worlds" of prediction 46 . Recent anatomical work has further refined this picture, revealing that the mouse cortico-basal ganglia-thalamic network comprises at least six parallel subnetworks–limbic, associative, and multiple somatomotor channels–each of which transduces a specific subset of cortical information through every elemental node of the loop and relays thalamic output back to the originating corticostriatal neurons in a bona fide closed loop 47 . These loops, however, are not hermetically sealed: open-loop projections, particularly from ventral striatum through medial SNr to motor thalamus, allow limbic information to exert a unidirectional influence on motor cortical output 48 , providing a circuit-level mechanism by which motivational and affective evaluations can bias action execution. Within this anatomical framework, the basal ganglia can be understood as implementing territory-specific objective functions : each striatal territory, embedded in a particular cortico-basal ganglia loop, optimizes a partially distinct behavioral objective using dopaminergic δ \delta computed over a loop-appropriate state representation. We can sketch this territory-by-territory as follows. Ventral striatum / limbic loops. The ventral striatum—particularly the nucleus accumbens core and shell—sits within limbic cortico-basal ganglia loops linking infralimbic, prelimbic, orbitofrontal, and insular cortices with mediodorsal and ventromedial thalamus 49 , 50 , 44 . Its dominant objective is to evaluate states in terms of motivational value, incentive salience, and homeostatic relevance, and to gate the initiation of approach or avoidance policies under affective and interoceptive control. Dopamine RPEs in ventral striatum are the best-characterized in the literature and exhibit the canonical pattern: bursts to unexpected reward, transfer to reward-predictive cues, and pauses at reward omission 8 . Value signals here reflect the subjective motivational significance of predicted outcomes, integrating information about reward magnitude, probability, delay, and—in the distributional account—the full shape of the outcome distribution 33 , 35 . In the multi-timescale framework, ventral striatal dopamine dynamics operate on the longest temporal horizons, integrating reward history over many trials and reflecting the shallowest temporal discounting 41 . In active-inference terms, ventral striatum helps convert prior preferences over outcomes into the expected free energy computations that bias policy selection toward preferred states 4 . Dorsomedial striatum / associative loops. The dorsomedial striatum (roughly, the primate caudate head) receives input from prefrontal, anterior cingulate, and parietal association cortices and participates in loops through mediodorsal and ventral anterior thalamus 49 , 2 , 44 . Its dominant objective is flexible, goal-directed action selection: learning which action produces which outcome under current task contingencies and updating that mapping when contingencies change 51 , 28 . Dopamine RPEs in dorsomedial striatum track action-outcome contingencies, show sensitivity to model-based value computations, and operate on intermediate temporal horizons 41 , 23 . Lesion and inactivation studies confirm that the dorsomedial striatum is necessary for goal-directed behavior that is sensitive to outcome devaluation and contingency degradation 51 . Dorsolateral striatum / sensorimotor loops. The dorsolateral striatum (roughly, the primate putamen) receives input from primary motor, premotor, and somatosensory cortices and projects through ventrolateral thalamus back to motor cortical areas 49 , 2 , 44 . Its dominant objective is the compilation and execution of well-practiced action sequences—skills, habits, and automatized motor programs that are relatively insensitive to immediate outcome devaluation 52 . Dopamine dynamics here are the fastest, integrating reward history over the fewest trials and reflecting the steepest temporal discounting 41 . Strikingly, dopamine signals in dorsolateral striatum are positively shifted relative to other regions: reward responses remain non-negative even for outcomes worse than expected, and clear inhibitory "dips" to negative prediction errors are absent or strongly attenuated 23 . This positive bias means that the effective teaching signal in dorsolateral striatum preferentially stamps in actions that were at least minimally successful, without erasing them when outcomes occasionally fall short—a property that may explain why dorsolateral-striatum-dependent behaviors are resistant to extinction and insensitive to devaluation, the hallmarks of habit 23 . Tail of the striatum / posterior sensorimotor territories. The tail of the striatum receives input from visual and auditory association cortices and is innervated by a distinct population of dopamine neurons that respond to novel and salient sensory stimuli with opposite initialization compared to ventral striatum–showing excitation rather than the typical inhibition to novel cues 53 , 54 . These posterior dopamine neurons have been shown to reinforce avoidance of threatening stimuli and, in recent work, to support value-free repetition biases and action-prediction-error signals, suggesting a role in stabilizing stimulus-action mappings distinct from canonical value-based learning 55 , 56 , 57 . Importantly, this territory-specific architecture again implies that the BG do not gate only overt motor actions. Through associative and limbic loops, the basal ganglia also gate cognitive operations–including attentional set-shifting, working-memory updating, and the selection of internal cognitive strategies. In active-inference accounts, these covert operations are themselves "actions" selected under the same policy-optimization logic that governs overt behavior: the basal ganglia bias which cortical content gains access to downstream processing, and dopaminergic δ \delta trains these gating policies just as it trains motor ones 5 , 6 , 42 . The ventral and dorsomedial loops, in particular, are positioned to gate prefrontal representations that determine what cognitive "policy" is currently active–whether to maintain a working-memory trace, switch attentional focus, or retrieve a new goal from long-term memory. On this view, the basal ganglia are best regarded as comparatively shallow evaluative and gating circuitry sitting downstream of richer cortical and hippocampal computations. Recent work increasingly supports a division of labor in which cortical inputs to striatum carry belief-like or predictive representations, and corticostriatal plasticity learns the mapping from those representations to values and action preferences; in recurrent-network terms, the striatum may function as the final readout stage of a deeper value-approximation architecture rather than as the site where the full task model is explicitly constructed 3 , 58 . This framing preserves what is distinctive about basal ganglia function–their role in evaluation, competitive gating, and dopaminergic credit assignment–while dissolving the misleading claim that they are intrinsically "model-free". What differs across ventral, associative, and sensorimotor loops is how rich the incoming state representation is, how far into the future it predicts, and what behavioral objective that territory is trying to optimize. From raw rewards to subjective utility The reward term in the basal ganglia’s TD objective should not be interpreted as raw physical payoff. What matters for action selection is the subjective utility of an outcome: the internally transformed desirability of reward after amount has been warped by the organism’s preferences, uncertainty attitude, temporal discounting, effort costs, reward type, and current physiological state. In that sense, the dopaminergic teaching signal is better described as a utility prediction error than as an error over objective reward amount. A useful shorthand is δ t u = u ( o t ) + γ V u ( s t + 1 ) − V u ( s t ) \begin{equation}
  \delta_t^u=u(o_t)+\gamma V^u(s_{t+1})-V^u(s_t)
\end{equation} where u ( o t ) u(o_t) denotes the subjectively transformed outcome and V u V^u is a value function expressed in utility coordinates. This is the right computational interpretation if the basal ganglia are to optimize behavior rather than merely track stimulus physics 59 , 60 . A broad body of work supports this stronger reading. Dopamine prediction-error responses integrate subjective value across multiple reward dimensions rather than encoding any single physical attribute in isolation: in monkeys, responses follow revealed-preference value integrated from reward amount, risk, and reward type, even when options cannot be ranked on any single objective scale 61 . Reward delays similarly reduce dopaminergic cue responses in parallel with behavioral temporal discounting 62 . And physiological need can rapidly revalue an outcome, with mesolimbic dopamine updating accordingly, as when sodium depletion turns hypertonic salt from aversive into appetitive 63 . Taken together, these results imply that the teaching signal received by striatal plasticity is already expressed in behaviorally meaningful value units rather than in raw sensory units. The most direct formal evidence comes from experiments that estimated utility functions explicitly. Stauffer and colleagues derived nonlinear utility functions from monkeys’ risky choices and showed that dopamine reward responses followed the marginal utility of reward—that is, the local slope of the utility function—rather than simple reward magnitude 64 . More recently, Hill and colleagues used an auction-like task to obtain trial-by-trial measures of instantaneous subjective value, and found that dopamine neurons tracked fluctuations in bids even when the physical reward was held constant; conversely, similar bids for different reward amounts elicited similar dopamine responses 65 . These results are especially important for a loss-function account because they show that the dopaminergic error signal follows the animal’s momentary internal valuation, not just its long-run average preference over objective outcomes. The newest evidence pushes this further from “utility” in the narrow sense toward subjective prospect value . Ferrari-Toniolo, Seak, and Schultz showed in 2026 that primate dopamine neurons nonlinearly code both reward magnitude and probability, and that the population signal integrates utility with subjectively weighted probability in a way that closely matches each animal’s revealed choices 66 . In parallel, single-neuron recordings across reward circuitry—including dorsal and ventral striatum—have shown prospect-theory-like coding in which neuronal activity is captured by a multiplicative combination of utility and probability-weighting functions, not merely expected value 67 . Thus, under uncertainty, the relevant BG objective may be not merely expected reward 𝔼 [ r ] \mathbb{E}[r] , nor even classical expected utility p u ( m ) pu(m) , but a subjectively transformed prospect value w ( p ) u ( m ) w(p)u(m) , in which both reward magnitude and reward probability are nonlinearly warped by the organism’s preferences. For the basal ganglia, the conceptual consequence is straightforward: the scalar RPE is not “about reward” in any naive physical sense. It is about whether the obtained or expected outcome was better or worse than anticipated in the organism’s own subjective currency . That currency can incorporate risk attitude, delay, effort, physiological need, reward identity, and even distortions of probability itself. Crucially, this subjectivity is not constructed within the basal ganglia in isolation. Midbrain dopamine responses are shaped by the rich cortical and cortico-hippocampal representations that feed into the dopaminergic system—representations that already embed inferred latent states, contextual structure, predictive beliefs, and other consequences of a generative world model. In that sense, dopamine does not merely report the value of a physically specified reward, but the value of that outcome as interpreted through a model-based, inference-laden representation of the situation . The basal ganglia therefore learn policies that maximize subjective utility —or, under uncertainty, subjective prospect value —over the state space furnished by cortex. The strongest direct evidence for this claim comes from relatively simple appetitive and risky-choice tasks, so one should be cautious about overextending it to all forms of abstract or social valuation; but as a description of the loss term driving dopaminergic teaching and corticostriatal learning, “subjective utility” is now a much better approximation than “objective reward amount,” precisely because the dopaminergic error itself is already informed by a rich generative model of the world 59 , 65 , 66 , 68 , 69 , 70 . Constraints and regularizers on the BG loss The canonical BG objective is often written as the maximization of expected discounted or average reward, but that idealization is too thin for the biology. A better description is that the basal ganglia optimize a constrained or regularized policy objective: candidate actions are evaluated not only by their expected payoffs, but also by their energetic and opportunity costs, their representational complexity, their fit to current physiological need, and the circuit-level requirement that commitment be sparse and mutually consistent. Importantly, these constraints need not appear as separate explicit symbols in a single dopaminergic scalar. In the brain they can enter by reshaping state representations upstream of the BG, by altering local dopamine release within striatum, and by changing how BG output nuclei regulate competition and threshold. On this view, the effective BG loss is not “maximize reward” in the abstract, but “maximize constrained subjective utility” 71 , 72 , 73 , 74 . A first and probably most central regularizer is effort and energetic cost . Across motor and cognitive domains, recent reviews converge on the idea that striatal dopamine helps compute net utility rather than raw benefit alone, biasing control toward options whose expected gains justify their costs 71 , 72 . In influential computational interpretations of BG opponency, this cost-benefit balancing is implemented by partially opponent pathways: direct-pathway activity emphasizes prospective benefits, whereas indirect-pathway activity emphasizes countervailing costs, risks, or losses, so that BG output approximates a net-action score rather than a pure reward score 73 , 74 . This does not mean that D1 neurons are literally “benefit neurons” and D2 neurons literally “cost neurons” in every context; the biology is more mixed than that. But as a loss-function summary it captures an important truth: corticostriatal plasticity is trained toward actions whose returns exceed their costs , not toward actions with maximal reward magnitude irrespective of expense. Consistent with that framing, nucleus accumbens D2R neurons carry signals about prior unfavorable outcomes and can bias subsequent risky choice toward safer options, suggesting that loss- and cost-like information enters directly into the policy update machinery 75 . At the same time, effort is not always just a subtractive penalty. Recent work shows that the relationship between cost and value can be path-dependent: striatal dopamine release reflects not only reward magnitude but also sunk cost , meaning that the reward response can be amplified by how much has already been invested 76 . A striking 2026 extension showed that in the nucleus accumbens, high-effort rewards evoke rapid local acetylcholine release, which then augments reward-evoked dopamine via nicotinic receptors on dopamine axons; blocking this cholinergic amplification selectively blunts dopamine release in high-effort contexts and impairs effortful behavior while sparing low-effort consumption 77 . The implication for a BG loss-function account is subtle but important: effort sometimes acts as a regularizer that discourages costly actions ex ante , but once effort has been expended it can also revalue the obtained outcome ex post , increasing commitment to the hard-won reward. Thus “effort cost” is not only a penalty term subtracted from reward; in some contexts it is folded back into the reward term itself. A second regularizer is policy complexity . Real animals cannot implement arbitrarily rich state-action mappings, so policy optimization is constrained by finite informational and memory capacity. Resource-rational RL formalizes this as a compression cost on the policy—often expressed as the mutual information between states and actions—which penalizes overly specific mappings even when they would increase reward 78 . In mice, behavior in a perceptual decision task lay close to the optimal reward-complexity frontier, and the same work found that midbrain dopamine responses to reward outcomes were suppressed by policy cost, consistent with the idea that phasic teaching signals themselves may incorporate a complexity penalty 78 . This is especially relevant for a BG chapter because it suggests that dopaminergic learning may be regularized not only by what an action earns, but by how expensive it is—in an informational sense—to maintain the policy that produces it. Put differently, the BG may favor policies that are good enough and compressible over policies that are slightly better but too brittle, high-dimensional, or memory-hungry to implement robustly. How such a penalty is implemented is an open question, but it need not be hard-wired. A plausible implementation is that the regularizer is itself learned : rather than optimizing against a fixed complexity coefficient, the system may acquire an inductive bias toward compressible policies from the statistics of experience—an Occam’s razor discovered rather than imposed—together with an implicit self-model as a compute- and memory-bound agent for whom cheap, robust policies tend to transfer. On this view the complexity cost need not be computed by a dedicated module but can be folded directly into the teaching signal, consistent with the observation that dopamine responses are suppressed for costlier policies 78 . A third class of constraints is homeostatic and allostatic . From this perspective, the BG do not optimize reward in a vacuum; they optimize actions with respect to the organism’s current bodily needs and predicted future internal states. In homeostatic reinforcement learning, the rewarding value of an outcome is defined by how much it reduces deviation from an internal setpoint, making reward intrinsically state dependent 79 . More recent modeling has pushed this directly into BG terms by proposing that physiological state rescales dopaminergic teaching signals and, as covered in "From raw rewards to subjective utility", that the relevant prediction error is an error over utility , not objective reinforcement, where utility depends on both outcome and need 80 . The sodium-appetite literature provides a concrete demonstration: homeostatically regulated RL models can reproduce both state-dependent choice behavior and the corresponding modulation of nucleus accumbens dopamine responses by sodium deprivation and satiety 81 . A recent review generalizes this logic still further, arguing that primary rewards are generated by interoceptive processes and are fundamentally dependent on internal states and goals, so classical RL must be extended to explain how those state-dependent reward signals are themselves generated 82 . For the BG loss, the upshot is that physiological need is not a side variable sitting outside the objective; it helps define the objective in the first place. A fourth regularizer is selection itself : the basal ganglia do not merely score actions, they impose sparse commitment under conflict and uncertainty. In computational terms, the STN-GPe-GPi circuitry can be viewed as adding a penalty against premature, impulsive, or mutually incompatible action selection, effectively raising the amount of evidence needed before commitment when conflict is high. This idea has long been formalized as an STN-mediated increase in decision threshold or “hold your horses” signal 83 , 84 . Recent recordings and modeling sharpen that picture: human BG recordings show that theta dynamics in STN, GPe, and GPi differentially shape a collapsing decision boundary as a function of conflict and uncertainty, with STN theta associated with slower boundary collapse and thus more deliberate choice in high-conflict settings 85 . Complementing this, 4-Hz STN stimulation in Parkinson’s patients increases decision threshold, whereas standard ~130-Hz stimulation decreases it 86 . These findings imply that part of the BG objective is not about reward magnitude at all, but about how and when commitment occurs: the system regularizes policies toward temporally appropriate, non-impulsive selections that respect competition among alternatives. Taken together, these results suggest that the most useful formal summary of the BG loss is not a bare reward term, but something like expected subjective utility under cost, complexity, physiological, and commitment constraints . Some of these constraints warp the reward term itself—for example when internal state changes the utility of an outcome, or when expended effort amplifies the dopamine response to a reward. Others act more like classical regularizers on the learned policy—for example by penalizing excessive policy complexity or by raising decision thresholds under conflict. This richer picture does not displace the TD/RPE framework; it specifies the kind of value function the TD machinery is actually learning. The scalar or population-level dopaminergic error remains the teaching signal, but it is computed around a value landscape that has already been reshaped by body state, cortical inference, local striatal modulation, and BG selection dynamics. In that sense, the basal ganglia do not optimize raw reward; they optimize a biologically feasible, organism-relative, and circuit-regularized approximation to long-run utility 71 , 78 , 81 , 85 . Relationship between BG loss and cortical loss The cortical and basal-ganglia loss functions are best understood as complementary rather than competing objectives . Cortex is primarily organized to reduce high-dimensional, precision-weighted prediction error—more generally, variational free energy—so as to infer latent causes, learn structured world models, and refine sensorimotor predictions. The basal ganglia, by contrast, do not mainly learn by reconstructing the sensory stream itself; they learn by assigning value to policies defined over the state representations that cortex, hippocampus, and other systems make available. Put simply, cortex is optimized for epistemic adequacy —“what state am I in, and what is likely to happen next?”—whereas the basal ganglia are optimized for pragmatic adequacy —“given that inferred state, which policy should be selected, energized, timed, or suppressed?” The difference is therefore not just one of anatomical locus but of computational object: cortical losses are locally factorized mismatches over features, levels, and timescales, whereas BG losses are comparatively low-bandwidth evaluative signals—classically TD/RPE-like errors—that say whether the current policy was better or worse than expected 87 , 88 , 3 , 4 . This division immediately clarifies why basal-ganglia learning can look strikingly “model-based” even if the BG themselves need not house a full generative world model. Dopaminergic and striatal evaluative errors are often computed over inferred states rather than raw observations. Hidden-state inference, contextual disambiguation, task structure, and belief-state estimation can all be carried out upstream by cortical, orbitofrontal, hippocampal, and prefrontal circuits; the BG then evaluate those inferred states using TD-like machinery. In that sense, the sophistication of the BG loss landscape is representation-dependent . A richer cortical state space gives the basal ganglia a richer object over which to compute subjective utility, risk, temporal horizon, and action value; a poorer cortical state space makes the very same dopaminergic machinery look more purely “model-free.” The crucial point is that much of the apparent model-basedness of BG optimization is imported through cortical state construction rather than generated de novo within the striatum 68 , 69 , 70 , 19 . The relationship is not merely serial but recurrent . Cortical inference determines the state features over which basal-ganglia value functions and action preferences are learned, but BG output feeds back through thalamocortical loops to regulate which actions, task sets, attentional allocations, working-memory gates, and internal operations are actually disinhibited. The BG therefore do not simply read out a value from a finished cortical model; they help determine which trajectories through state space the organism will actually traverse. By selecting a saccade, a reach, a retrieval policy, or a cognitive set, the BG alter the future sensory samples the organism receives and therefore alter the subsequent cortical prediction errors available for learning. Cortex and BG are thus better described as a coupled optimizer pair : cortex constructs and updates the predictive state space, while the basal ganglia compress that space into policy biases, vigor settings, and commitment signals that determine how the agent samples the world next 46 , 3 . In an active-inference vocabulary, this complementarity can be stated even more tightly. Cortical losses are naturally formulated in terms of variational free energy and its forward-looking extension, expected free energy, which score both current model fit and the anticipated epistemic and pragmatic consequences of future policies. Within that broader architecture, the basal ganglia can be understood as implementing a comparatively low-bandwidth policy-selection and policy-precision mechanism embedded within a larger cortical generative model. Cortex—especially temporally deep hippocampal and prefrontal circuitry—contributes the hidden-state model and predicted outcome landscape; dopaminergic/BG circuits help transform those predictions and preferences into competitive biases over executable policies. On this reading, classical RPE-based BG losses are not alien to cortical free-energy minimization. They are one biologically efficient way of approximating the evaluative part of policy optimization once a cortical generative model has already furnished the relevant state space and predicted consequences 89 , 4 , 90 . The boundary, however, is functional rather than absolute . Dopamine is not only a corticostriatal teaching signal; it also modulates cortical processing, and cortical circuits in turn help shape dopaminergic responses through state inference, uncertainty estimation, and model-based prediction. The cleanest synthesis, then, is not that cortex “learns” while BG merely “choose,” but that cortex and BG implement distinct yet reciprocally nested loss functions . Cortex primarily minimizes errors of representation, prediction, and uncertainty over a structured generative model of the world. The basal ganglia primarily minimize errors of valuation, policy selection, timing, and commitment over the states that model provides. The former builds the space of possibilities; the latter assigns actional significance to that space. Together they form a two-level optimization architecture in which world-model learning and policy learning are analytically separable, but biologically inseparable in operation 91 , 92 , 93 .
